WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:05.840
- Welcome everybody to the seminar today.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.670
It's a pleasure to welcome Marshall Burke,

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:09.260
who is an associate professor

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:11.160
in the Department of Earth System Science

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:12.830
at Stanford University,

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:14.480
where he is also a deputy director

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.740
at the Center on Food
Security and the Environment.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:21.540
Marshall's research focuses
on social and economic impacts

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:22.790
of environmental change,

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:24.130
and on measuring and understanding

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:27.453
economic livelihoods across
the developing world.

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:29.810
I tend to think of Marshall's work

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:32.740
as focusing on big
questions using big data,

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:37.110
using empirical methods that
are very carefully executed.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:39.270
And one of the interesting
things about Marshall's research

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:41.600
is that he publishes both in economics

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.660
and scientific journals,

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:46.560
and he holds a PhD in
agricultural resource economics

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.130
from the University of
California, Berkeley.

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:50.810
It's a pleasure to welcome him here today,

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:52.570
he's gonna be speaking
to us on understanding

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:56.090
the causal impacts of
climate on human conflict.

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:58.670
And before I turn it over to
Marshall to start his seminar,

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:01.980
I'll just say that he welcomes
questions or interruptions,

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.978
clarifications or comments
throughout the seminar.

00:01:04.978 --> 00:01:07.840
You're welcome to just
raise your hand feature,

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:09.900
and he'll either see it and respond,

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.640
or I'll just give him a little nudge

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:12.970
to focus on your questions

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:15.020
and keep on with his presentation.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:17.100
So without any further
introduction, welcome Marshall

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.710
and we look forward to your presentation.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:21.220
- Great. Thanks a lot, Matt.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:22.840
And thank you guys for having me,

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.820
too bad couldn't make it in person,

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:26.600
hopefully sometime in the future,

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.029
but thanks a lot for coming

00:01:28.029 --> 00:01:32.520
on what I hear is a very rainy,
blustery day in New Haven.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.023
So let me share some slides.

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:37.790
Yeah, so as Matt said,

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:40.080
this is very much work in progress

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.070
and would love to get your feedback

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:44.240
and your comments and
questions at any point.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.710
So please do jump in and interrupt.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:48.940
It might be hard for
me to see the hands up,

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:52.260
so please be aggressive in intervening.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:55.130
I'll try to also leave
some time at the end

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:57.070
for additional questions and conversation.

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:59.940
Cause I think there's a lot of
ways we could take this work.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:02.890
So as Matt said, this
is going to be a talk,

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:04.340
and again, really work in progress,

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:07.700
where we are trying to
take a big picture look

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:11.480
at the relationship
between changes in climate,

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:12.910
broadly construed,

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:16.520
although today we'll focus
mainly on temperature changes,

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:19.430
which are perhaps the best
studied and best observed,

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:21.070
and its potential impacts

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:23.560
on different types of
conflict around the world.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:26.663
And I'll try to define these
more precisely in a moment,

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:30.180
but we are going to be casting a wide net,

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:31.800
both in terms of how
we think about climate

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.740
and in terms of how we
think about conflict.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.480
And we are going to be using data,

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.460
as much data as we can assemble,

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:42.820
either on our own or from
existing published studies

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:45.260
to try to characterize

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:47.730
whether there's an overall
relationship on average

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:50.560
and how that relationship
might differ across settings.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:53.570
And then to use that
backward looking evidence

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:54.490
from recent history,

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:56.952
to try to say something about
what the world might look like

00:02:56.952 --> 00:02:59.430
going forward as the climate warms

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.970
and as economies hopefully
continue to develop.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.900
So this is joint work with
two Berkeley collaborators,

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:09.330
Ted Miguel, and Sol Hsiang,
three, Joel Ferguson.

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:11.170
So Ted and Sol are on faculty there,

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:12.959
Joel Ferguson is a great graduate student

00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:14.090
in RE Department,

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:18.050
and Jerry Min is a RA who works with me,

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:19.330
here at Stanford.

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:23.180
Okay. So if I can advance my slides,

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:25.340
I think I'm speaking to
a crowd that thinks a lot

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.170
about conflict and its consequences.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:32.060
So maybe what my motivation here

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.690
will be a little bit
preaching to the choir,

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:36.580
but a lot of people don't
think a lot about conflict,

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:39.830
but they do read Steven
Pinker's popular press books.

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:43.180
These are very popular and
I'm sure many of you have read

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:45.730
this very nice book, "Better
angels of our nature",

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:48.100
subtitle, "Why violence has declined".

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:49.010
And in this book,

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:52.200
Pinker really takes a very long view,

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:56.380
century millennial scale
view on human violence,

00:03:56.380 --> 00:04:00.810
and argues, I would
say, fairly persuasively

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:02.220
that over the very long run,

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:05.480
we've seen a pretty remarkable decline

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:08.620
in humans being nasty towards one another,

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:10.070
violent outcomes.

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:12.160
And really what he can study well here

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:14.260
is more individual level conflict.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:16.560
You know, homicide, basically,

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.690
a lot of his evidence comes from homicide,

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:22.720
but also has relevance for
broader group scale conflict.

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:24.150
So a lot of people read
this book and think,

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:26.190
well, conflict is sort of
a thing of the past, right?

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:27.780
Yeah, maybe we still see some,

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:29.200
but it's gone down substantially

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:31.790
and we're much more peaceful than we were

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:34.403
decades, centuries, millennia ago.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:37.420
That might be true.

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:40.830
But what is also true is if
you look in a more recent era,

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:45.040
we have seen a not monotonic,

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:46.410
not completely steady,

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:49.570
but overall increase in
various types of conflicts.

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:53.020
So I apologize that the X
axis here is hard to read,

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:58.020
the data here go from the
post-war period in 1946 to 2020.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:00.220
And what you see in the
different colors here

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:04.490
are the number of
conflicts around the world

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:05.350
of various types.

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:07.560
So these have sort of
clinical definitions,

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:12.180
in the darker blue is what's
officially called a civil war.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:16.670
That is a conflict in which
a thousand people died

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:20.050
and where one of the
combatants is a state.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:22.960
So think of this as a state,

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:24.240
either fighting another state,

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:28.120
or more typically fighting
some other groups,

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:30.320
some other non-state
actor within the country.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:32.480
So the wars are the very large one

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:34.710
that kill over a thousand people,

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:37.270
and the lighter colored,
reddish ones here,

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:41.100
are minor ones that lead to
at least 25 battle deaths.

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.170
Often these are associated
with many more deaths

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:44.310
beyond just the battle deaths,

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:46.400
but this is how these are counted.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:47.900
And sort of no matter how you count these,

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:50.590
the number sort of peaked in the mid '90s,

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:55.110
after the end of Cold War
and then declined afterwards,

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:56.279
Steven Pinker wrote his book,

00:05:56.279 --> 00:05:57.560
but then in the last decade,

00:05:57.560 --> 00:05:59.260
these have come back
up pretty dramatically

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:03.280
and now have reached totals
that exceed what we saw

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:06.140
during the, really, the most
conflictual post-war period,

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:08.120
which was the early '90s in the world.

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:11.320
So now the annual number of
conflicts is at an all time high

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:12.710
post World War II.

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:14.820
So Pinker might be right on the long view,

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:15.653
but in the shorter view,

00:06:15.653 --> 00:06:17.340
conflict is certainly not going down,

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:19.423
if anything, it's going up.

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:23.440
You see conflicts around the world,

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:25.370
so this is not just
concentrated in one area.

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:27.470
Here's the map from 2020.

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:29.460
So these are data from the UCDP.

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:30.293
For whatever reason,

00:06:30.293 --> 00:06:33.370
the Scandinavians have
really committed themselves

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:35.190
to counting conflicts around the world,

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:37.470
so all our great data
come from different groups

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:39.220
in Norway and Sweden.

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:42.170
This is from the UCDP and
I'll show you various analysis

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:44.040
using the UCDP data.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:46.140
Here's just a recent figure they put out,

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:48.910
showing where these state-based
conflicts are happening,

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:49.743
really right now.

00:06:49.743 --> 00:06:50.780
So this is from last year.

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:52.670
Many of these are ongoing,
and as you can see,

00:06:52.670 --> 00:06:55.740
these are pretty widespread
around the world.

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:58.360
They're not just
concentrated in one region.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:02.350
So conflict is going up,
spread around the world,

00:07:02.350 --> 00:07:05.860
we are in a high conflict
period, spread down.

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:08.449
And again, I think for this crew,

00:07:08.449 --> 00:07:10.450
this stuff is well known,

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:13.080
but conflict is bad on its own,

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:16.400
but it's also bad because
it worsens many outcomes

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:17.320
that we care about.

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:20.120
So if you think about the
number of forced displacement,

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:23.640
conflict is a leading cause
of forced displacement

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:24.536
around the world,

00:07:24.536 --> 00:07:27.250
and no surprises, conflicts have increased

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:28.090
in the last decade.

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:30.460
We've seen a steady increase
in the number of people,

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:31.840
either internally displaced

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:35.160
or crossing borders to escape conflicts.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:37.260
Again, this is not just conflict driven,

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:40.770
but conflict has been a large driver here.

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:42.170
You can also think of other outcomes,

00:07:42.170 --> 00:07:44.450
economic outcomes or
food insecurity outcomes.

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:48.560
So food insecurity or the
prevalence of undernutrition

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:51.400
was on a steady decline for many years,

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:53.060
again, until about a decade ago,

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:55.350
and now is flattened down,

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:56.660
and if anything, is on the rise,

00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:59.600
the last year here is
mainly COVID related.

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:01.320
But a lot of this
flattening, I think, again,

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:05.591
can be attributed to the
increase in conflict.

00:08:05.591 --> 00:08:08.870
It's reversing the
progress that we've seen

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:12.490
against food insecurity around the world.

00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:13.690
Okay. So that's conflict.

00:08:13.690 --> 00:08:18.420
So why might climate be
at all relevant here?

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:19.960
Let me motivate this in a couple of ways,

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:23.020
first, by using some
quotes from luminaries

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:24.510
from the recent and distant past.

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:28.700
So here's president Barack
Obama when he got elected

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:31.050
and then immediately won
the Nobel peace prize.

00:08:31.970 --> 00:08:33.507
Here's what he said during that speech,

00:08:33.507 --> 00:08:35.697
"the world must come together
to confront climate change.

00:08:35.697 --> 00:08:38.387
"There is little scientific
dispute that if we do nothing,

00:08:38.387 --> 00:08:40.167
"we will face more drought, more famine,

00:08:40.167 --> 00:08:41.377
"more mass displacement,

00:08:41.377 --> 00:08:43.560
"all of which will fuel
more conflict for decades."

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:46.390
So here is Obama over a decade ago,

00:08:46.390 --> 00:08:48.670
making a very strong empirical claim

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:52.342
relating changes in climate
to conflict outcomes.

00:08:52.342 --> 00:08:53.713
We can go back further,

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:58.470
if I can advance my slide,
here we go, Romeo and Juliet.

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:00.493
So now we're going back centuries.

00:09:01.612 --> 00:09:02.840
If you remember Romeo and Juliet,

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:05.010
roughly there's the good
guys and the bad guys,

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:08.230
here the set of good guys
talking to each other,

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:09.370
Benvolio and Mercutio,

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:11.390
these are good friends of Romeo.

00:09:11.390 --> 00:09:13.690
This is midway through Romeo and Juliet.

00:09:13.690 --> 00:09:16.950
So they're out in the street
and it's a very hot day.

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:18.960
So, "I pray the good
Mercutio, let's retire."

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:19.793
Let's go inside.

00:09:19.793 --> 00:09:23.737
"The day is hot." The
Capulets, the bad guys, abroad,

00:09:23.737 --> 00:09:25.817
"and if we meet, we
shall not 'scape a brawl,

00:09:25.817 --> 00:09:28.900
"for now these hot days is
the mad blood stirring."

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:31.280
So basically Shakespeare is saying here,

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:33.313
there's just something about it being hot

00:09:33.313 --> 00:09:37.360
that causes us to want
to fight each other.

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:39.260
And sure enough, they don't go inside,

00:09:40.439 --> 00:09:43.780
all hell breaks loose, many people die.

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:46.690
And so Romeo and Juliet,
and Shakespeare in general,

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:50.330
is typically not read as empirical work

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:51.610
on climate and conflict yet.

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:54.460
I would argue there's
some early evidence here,

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:56.010
in Romeo and Juliet.

00:09:56.010 --> 00:09:58.870
Okay. So why might this actually matter?

00:09:58.870 --> 00:10:03.700
So if we go back to these
quotes, you can see Obama,

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:07.060
I would say making perhaps
an economic argument

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:10.210
between changes in conditions,
economic conditions,

00:10:10.210 --> 00:10:11.960
livelihood conditions on the ground,

00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:14.130
and a propensity for conflict.

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:16.430
So that's one potential mechanism

00:10:16.430 --> 00:10:18.140
that might link climate and conflict.

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:20.930
So if we think changes in
climate affect livelihoods,

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:22.420
and we think changes in livelihoods

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:24.090
affect the propensity for conflict,

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:26.390
then you could see a natural pathway

00:10:26.390 --> 00:10:27.640
from climate to conflict.

00:10:28.519 --> 00:10:30.170
Shakespeare is making a
very different argument.

00:10:30.170 --> 00:10:32.540
It's not about economic conditions here.

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:33.530
It's just something about

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:37.570
the physiological response to heat.

00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:40.400
And I think if we introspect a little bit

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:43.190
and you just think about
how you feel on a hot day,

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:44.683
potentially grumpier,

00:10:46.090 --> 00:10:49.486
you might think, can that lead to changes

00:10:49.486 --> 00:10:51.510
in certain types of conflict outcomes,

00:10:51.510 --> 00:10:53.120
just a physiological response.

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:54.200
And I'll show you some evidence

00:10:54.200 --> 00:10:56.293
that actually that might be the case.

00:10:57.296 --> 00:10:58.930
So there are multiple mechanisms.

00:10:58.930 --> 00:11:00.870
So again, the Obama story is perhaps

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:02.050
about economic conditions,

00:11:02.050 --> 00:11:04.120
the Shakespearian story is perhaps about

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:07.770
sort of physiological or
psychological response.

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:08.810
There are other stories,

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:11.050
and again, I'll get to the
literature in a second,

00:11:11.050 --> 00:11:13.930
that has looked at this one,
is more a migration story,

00:11:13.930 --> 00:11:17.230
so maybe relevant to the research of folks

00:11:17.230 --> 00:11:19.053
at the McMillan Center here.

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:21.610
So climate change can drive migration,

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:24.647
there's good empirical evidence of that.

00:11:24.647 --> 00:11:26.780
And there are many studies suggesting that

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:30.020
migrants, in various ways,

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:31.480
as they come into new areas,

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:34.950
can perhaps precipitate conflict

00:11:34.950 --> 00:11:39.680
with people in the receiving location.

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:42.390
Another long-term story
is about grievances,

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:46.030
so perhaps changes in climate can amplify

00:11:46.030 --> 00:11:49.140
or multiply existing
grievances between groups,

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:52.700
as they alter, again, economic
conditions on the ground.

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:53.950
So that's yet another story

00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:55.990
why climate might matter for conflict.

00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:57.310
Differentiating these stories

00:11:57.310 --> 00:11:59.430
is something we're gonna
try to do in this work,

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:01.960
and I'll get back to that towards the end.

00:12:01.960 --> 00:12:02.793
Okay.

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:06.510
I wanna emphasize from the very beginning

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:08.550
that we are not environmental
determinists here,

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:10.870
nothing we're going to study empirically

00:12:10.870 --> 00:12:13.500
links between changes
in climate and conflict.

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:16.520
None of this should argue
that climate is the only,

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:19.710
nor the most important
"cause of conflict".

00:12:19.710 --> 00:12:21.590
Clearly there are many other things

00:12:21.590 --> 00:12:24.550
that determine whether or
not a conflict happens.

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:28.700
We are going to try to
isolate the climate component

00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:31.060
of these causes.

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:33.350
So again, we're not
being determinists here,

00:12:33.350 --> 00:12:36.170
we're just trying to
understand empirically,

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:38.110
is climate an important driver

00:12:38.110 --> 00:12:41.010
of observed conflict that we see.

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:42.440
Okay.

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:45.743
And so I wanna be very clear about this.

00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:48.880
But it could be an important,

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:50.350
and basically this is an exercise

00:12:50.350 --> 00:12:52.400
to try to understand how important it is.

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:55.540
The Defense Department
now talks about climate

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:56.640
as a threat multiplier.

00:12:56.640 --> 00:12:58.660
So again, there are many existing threats,

00:12:58.660 --> 00:13:00.360
you can see sort of between the aerosphere

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:02.670
and changes in climate might alter

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:05.060
or magnify those threats.

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:07.540
So policy makers are using this language,

00:13:07.540 --> 00:13:09.780
and this could have specific implications

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:12.020
for interventions that we might undertake.

00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:13.660
If climate is an important cause,

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:15.510
that might change actually
what we do on the ground

00:13:15.510 --> 00:13:18.710
to prevent future
conflicts from happening.

00:13:18.710 --> 00:13:20.510
So that's why we're gonna study it.

00:13:20.510 --> 00:13:21.840
Okay, so what do we
actually know about this?

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:23.010
So it turns out there's actually

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:25.590
a very large literature on this,

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:27.530
and it's really accelerated
in the last decade.

00:13:27.530 --> 00:13:32.040
So, with Sol and Ted
almost a decade ago now,

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:33.890
we did what we thought it was

00:13:33.890 --> 00:13:36.040
the first sort of empirical meta analysis

00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:37.130
of the existing literature.

00:13:37.130 --> 00:13:40.270
Back then there was a few dozen studies

00:13:40.270 --> 00:13:43.530
and here we're really focused
on the quantitative studies,

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:48.120
studies that used longitudinal
data for the most part

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:50.350
to study empirical relationships

00:13:50.350 --> 00:13:52.400
between climate and conflict.

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:55.290
And so this was published, again, in 2013,

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:59.320
and we argued, was the sort
of best empirical summary

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:01.913
of relationships in the literature.

00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:04.870
So here, in this study,

00:14:04.870 --> 00:14:06.610
and in the update that I'm gonna show you,

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:08.090
we really focused on settings

00:14:08.090 --> 00:14:10.470
where we can isolate the impact of climate

00:14:10.470 --> 00:14:13.830
from all the other potential
causes of conflict.

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:17.250
And so actually doing this
in a meta analytic way

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:19.360
requires going back to the primary data

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:20.320
in many of these studies,

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:23.600
and reanalyzing them in
what we would think of

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:25.400
as statistically responsible ways.

00:14:25.400 --> 00:14:27.160
Using a statistical methodology

00:14:27.160 --> 00:14:28.286
that I'll talk about in a second,

00:14:28.286 --> 00:14:30.970
that again, can really
isolate the impact of climate

00:14:30.970 --> 00:14:34.283
from all the other things that
could be causing conflict.

00:14:35.470 --> 00:14:37.610
So the findings from this
earlier meta analysis, again,

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:38.833
the few dozen studies,

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:43.310
we found that increases in temperatures

00:14:43.310 --> 00:14:44.830
and an extreme precipitation,

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:47.090
but again, the temperature
signal seemed the strongest,

00:14:47.090 --> 00:14:49.910
led to increases in both
interpersonal conflict,

00:14:49.910 --> 00:14:53.210
think of this as violent assault,

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:55.500
or homicide, in the worst case outcome.

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:58.030
So two people having an incompatibility

00:14:58.030 --> 00:15:00.360
and a larger increase
in inter-group conflict.

00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:03.240
So think of this as the
civil complex, civil wars,

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:05.600
that I showed you before.

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:09.470
So robust statistically
significant average relationships

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:12.360
between climate and conflicts

00:15:14.470 --> 00:15:16.322
across the studies that exist.

00:15:16.322 --> 00:15:19.300
So that was almost 10 years ago now.

00:15:19.300 --> 00:15:21.480
There has been an immense
amount of work on this

00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:25.730
in the last eight or 10 years
since we published that,

00:15:25.730 --> 00:15:27.840
so we want to revisit
this earlier analysis,

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:30.296
we wanna understand
whether our earlier results

00:15:30.296 --> 00:15:33.150
still hold up, given the, again,

00:15:33.150 --> 00:15:35.400
the large amount that's
been published since.

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:38.170
I think what's interesting

00:15:38.170 --> 00:15:40.090
and what made us want to revisit this

00:15:40.090 --> 00:15:43.630
is not only that, but that
many new types of conflict

00:15:43.630 --> 00:15:46.133
and violence around the
world are being studied.

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:49.400
So I can show you some results on that.

00:15:49.400 --> 00:15:50.707
And because we have a lot more literature

00:15:50.707 --> 00:15:51.690
and a lot more data,

00:15:51.690 --> 00:15:54.820
we have new capabilities and opportunities

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:57.160
to understand the mechanism
linking climate and conflict.

00:15:57.160 --> 00:16:00.377
That was not something we
could do a very good job with

00:16:00.377 --> 00:16:01.860
in the earlier study.

00:16:01.860 --> 00:16:03.300
And of course, that's very important

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:05.560
if we wanna think about interventions.

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:06.460
And finally, again,

00:16:06.460 --> 00:16:09.370
we've had a decade with
a lot more conflict

00:16:09.370 --> 00:16:11.440
and a decade where we've
seen lots of warming, right?

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:12.500
So you would think it's a decade

00:16:12.500 --> 00:16:14.720
where we might be able to
learn a lot empirically

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:17.810
about the relationship
between climate broadly

00:16:17.810 --> 00:16:19.173
and conflict.

00:16:20.270 --> 00:16:22.760
Okay. So here's what we're
gonna do in this study.

00:16:22.760 --> 00:16:25.900
Again, this is mainly a meta analysis,

00:16:25.900 --> 00:16:28.690
although we are gonna do some
primary data work as well.

00:16:28.690 --> 00:16:32.660
So we are going to update
our earlier meta analysis

00:16:32.660 --> 00:16:36.090
on what we will call inter-group conflict,

00:16:36.090 --> 00:16:38.670
so again, think of these
as groups fighting groups,

00:16:38.670 --> 00:16:41.549
this is civil conflicts of a war,

00:16:41.549 --> 00:16:44.850
as well as some other outcomes,
I'll show you in a moment.

00:16:44.850 --> 00:16:45.840
So we're gonna distinguish that

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:47.090
from interpersonal conflict,

00:16:47.090 --> 00:16:50.980
these are individuals
potentially harming one another,

00:16:50.980 --> 00:16:52.533
homicide, violent assault.

00:16:53.660 --> 00:16:57.160
We are also going to add in a
third category of self-harm.

00:16:57.160 --> 00:16:58.180
And so this is,

00:16:58.180 --> 00:17:00.070
you could think of
instead of interpersonal,

00:17:00.070 --> 00:17:03.160
intrapersonal conflict.

00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:05.360
There's been a lot of
work on that recently,

00:17:05.360 --> 00:17:06.630
I'll show you that.

00:17:06.630 --> 00:17:08.560
Number three, we're gonna
dive deeper into mechanism

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:10.750
and I'll show you what
we've gotten done on that.

00:17:10.750 --> 00:17:12.380
We're far from having a full story here.

00:17:12.380 --> 00:17:13.980
I'm gonna look at one potential mechanism

00:17:13.980 --> 00:17:16.230
that we can observe and try to measure.

00:17:16.230 --> 00:17:18.140
And finally, and I
won't get to this today,

00:17:18.140 --> 00:17:20.573
we wanna think about
potential climate impacts,

00:17:21.650 --> 00:17:22.550
both in understanding

00:17:22.550 --> 00:17:24.710
what climate scientists call attribution,

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:26.540
so what of the existing,

00:17:26.540 --> 00:17:30.410
the changes that we've
already observed in conflict,

00:17:30.410 --> 00:17:32.793
what can be attributed
to changes in climate,

00:17:33.740 --> 00:17:35.920
and second would be a projection
of forward looking thing.

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:38.920
So what might we think
would happen in the future,

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:41.560
given what we've observed
in the recent past,

00:17:41.560 --> 00:17:43.350
as temperatures continue to warm.

00:17:43.350 --> 00:17:44.560
So I won't do either of those today,

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:45.680
I'll sort of try to set that up,

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:48.983
but that part of the
analysis is not complete.

00:17:50.230 --> 00:17:51.063
- Marshall, Marshall,

00:17:51.063 --> 00:17:52.670
can I just jump in with a quick question?

00:17:52.670 --> 00:17:53.929
- Yeah, please.

00:17:53.929 --> 00:17:55.330
- Maybe you're gonna get to this,

00:17:55.330 --> 00:17:56.820
but one just thing I wonder,

00:17:56.820 --> 00:17:59.130
and I thought about this with
the first slide that you had

00:17:59.130 --> 00:18:01.810
with the trend in conflict over time,

00:18:01.810 --> 00:18:05.750
just over time, to what extent
does the data that we know

00:18:05.750 --> 00:18:08.720
about the increased prevalence of violence

00:18:08.720 --> 00:18:12.000
relate to just more comprehensive
and sophisticated ways

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:13.293
of collecting data?

00:18:14.130 --> 00:18:15.280
- Yeah, it's a great question.

00:18:15.280 --> 00:18:17.680
So one thing you would
very much be worried about

00:18:17.680 --> 00:18:20.780
is time trends for various reasons,

00:18:20.780 --> 00:18:25.120
but one just a recording
artifact, basically.

00:18:25.120 --> 00:18:28.850
We have better data on where
and when conflicts occur,

00:18:28.850 --> 00:18:31.420
and so we record more of them over time.

00:18:31.420 --> 00:18:32.570
Yeah, that's absolutely right,

00:18:32.570 --> 00:18:35.550
and I think that is definitely part

00:18:35.550 --> 00:18:36.510
of some of these datasets.

00:18:36.510 --> 00:18:39.365
So the way many of these
climate event data sets work

00:18:39.365 --> 00:18:43.250
is they scrape basically news reports.

00:18:43.250 --> 00:18:44.870
So they look at local news reports,

00:18:44.870 --> 00:18:46.930
either from media organizations,

00:18:46.930 --> 00:18:49.370
from humanitarian organizations,

00:18:49.370 --> 00:18:53.050
and then code these
events as conflicts or not

00:18:53.050 --> 00:18:55.940
using existing data that
they can find or estimate

00:18:55.940 --> 00:18:58.390
or put together on how
many people were affected.

00:18:59.756 --> 00:19:02.810
And it has to be the
case that our information

00:19:02.810 --> 00:19:04.530
from local news organizations,

00:19:04.530 --> 00:19:06.340
or from humanitarian organizations,

00:19:06.340 --> 00:19:09.470
has improved over time
leading to some of that trend.

00:19:09.470 --> 00:19:13.570
So we need to be very careful
about that exact problem,

00:19:13.570 --> 00:19:15.680
and think about how to control

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:18.990
for the possibility of an
overall secular increase

00:19:18.990 --> 00:19:21.970
just due to reporting.

00:19:21.970 --> 00:19:24.770
And so in the analysis, and
then I'm gonna show you,

00:19:24.770 --> 00:19:27.350
what we can do is try to control

00:19:27.350 --> 00:19:29.490
either across all the data,

00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:31.140
or in sort of a local way

00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:34.990
for any sort of secular, what
we would call, time trends.

00:19:34.990 --> 00:19:39.270
Anything else that's driving
trends in conflict over time

00:19:39.270 --> 00:19:40.280
that might not be climate,

00:19:40.280 --> 00:19:42.040
and again, reporting is one of the things

00:19:42.040 --> 00:19:42.960
we're gonna worry about, yeah.

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:44.750
So you're exactly right,

00:19:44.750 --> 00:19:47.050
and hopefully we're doing a good job

00:19:47.050 --> 00:19:49.923
trying to account for
the reporting part of it.

00:19:51.309 --> 00:19:55.180
Okay, so again, this is
a quantitative analysis.

00:19:55.180 --> 00:19:56.751
We are not gonna focus on a very large

00:19:56.751 --> 00:19:58.990
and important case study in literature,

00:19:58.990 --> 00:20:00.400
thinking about conflict.

00:20:00.400 --> 00:20:03.110
We are going to focus,
for better or worse,

00:20:03.110 --> 00:20:05.040
on quantitative studies that, again,

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:09.210
statistically relate
changes in conflict outcomes

00:20:09.210 --> 00:20:12.180
to changes in observed climate.

00:20:12.180 --> 00:20:14.040
And in particular,

00:20:14.040 --> 00:20:15.760
we are going to focus on studies

00:20:15.760 --> 00:20:18.850
that can observe fixed
populations over time

00:20:18.850 --> 00:20:19.990
as the climate fluctuates.

00:20:19.990 --> 00:20:24.990
So a standard example here, perhaps,

00:20:25.050 --> 00:20:29.940
so you might think of, let's
compare Norway to Nigeria.

00:20:29.940 --> 00:20:34.330
So Norway is historically
been fairly peaceful,

00:20:34.330 --> 00:20:36.600
at least in the past half century,

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:37.950
go back further, less the case,

00:20:37.950 --> 00:20:42.950
but thinking in recent era
Norway is fairly peaceful,

00:20:43.090 --> 00:20:45.540
Nigeria has unfortunately
experienced a lot of conflict.

00:20:45.540 --> 00:20:47.020
Norway tends to be quite cool,

00:20:47.020 --> 00:20:48.480
Nigeria is quite hot.

00:20:48.480 --> 00:20:50.670
So a naive analysis would say,

00:20:50.670 --> 00:20:53.060
you get more conflict where it's hot,

00:20:53.060 --> 00:20:56.050
because Nigeria has more
conflict than Norway.

00:20:56.050 --> 00:20:57.900
Obviously that's not a great comparison.

00:20:57.900 --> 00:20:59.540
We might think that Norway and Nigeria

00:20:59.540 --> 00:21:01.230
differ in all sorts of other ways,

00:21:01.230 --> 00:21:03.390
other than just their average temperature.

00:21:03.390 --> 00:21:06.360
And so that could be a
very confounded comparison.

00:21:06.360 --> 00:21:09.150
So we do not include studies

00:21:09.150 --> 00:21:11.320
that make those sorts of
cross-sectional comparisons.

00:21:11.320 --> 00:21:13.711
Instead, we want to use studies that

00:21:13.711 --> 00:21:15.880
don't compare Norway to Nigeria,

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:18.500
but compare Nigeria or some other location

00:21:18.500 --> 00:21:19.780
to itself over time,

00:21:19.780 --> 00:21:22.760
as it experiences hotter versus cooler

00:21:22.760 --> 00:21:25.083
or wetter versus dryer years.

00:21:26.870 --> 00:21:30.560
So again, we're going
to use within location

00:21:30.560 --> 00:21:32.363
variation over time,

00:21:33.310 --> 00:21:37.150
and controlling, as Matt brought up,

00:21:37.150 --> 00:21:39.800
for any other thing that could be trending

00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:42.530
in a common way across
all units over time.

00:21:42.530 --> 00:21:45.330
So if we think this
sort of reporting effect

00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:48.740
is sort of similar across
a lot of different units,

00:21:48.740 --> 00:21:50.120
let's say all countries in Africa

00:21:50.120 --> 00:21:51.770
sort of saw an increase in conflict

00:21:51.770 --> 00:21:55.840
just because we're now reporting
on more conflict in Africa.

00:21:55.840 --> 00:21:57.430
We're gonna sort of control for that

00:21:57.430 --> 00:22:00.060
by accounting for an Africa wide trend,

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:04.350
or an Africa wide year fixed
effect in the parlance here.

00:22:04.350 --> 00:22:07.480
So the sort of technology
we're gonna be using here,

00:22:07.480 --> 00:22:08.580
the statistical technology

00:22:08.580 --> 00:22:10.620
is panel fixed effects regression.

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:12.150
I didn't put up any equations here,

00:22:12.150 --> 00:22:14.500
more than happy to go
into the details on this.

00:22:15.500 --> 00:22:17.600
And the comparisons that come out of these

00:22:17.600 --> 00:22:19.850
are basically the ones
that are on the slide.

00:22:20.840 --> 00:22:23.220
There are other things you want
to do in these regressions.

00:22:23.220 --> 00:22:25.000
So we're actually gonna
push the data pretty hard.

00:22:25.000 --> 00:22:26.500
We want to account for

00:22:26.500 --> 00:22:29.420
potential lag effects
of temperature as well.

00:22:29.420 --> 00:22:30.770
Those could go in two directions,

00:22:30.770 --> 00:22:32.300
it could be the case that

00:22:32.300 --> 00:22:34.620
multiple years of
negative climate exposure

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:35.880
increases conflict,

00:22:35.880 --> 00:22:38.440
so you want to account for both current

00:22:38.440 --> 00:22:40.500
and previous exposure.

00:22:40.500 --> 00:22:43.760
It's also the case that
a hot year last year

00:22:43.760 --> 00:22:46.850
could've caused a conflict to occur

00:22:46.850 --> 00:22:49.790
that was already going to occur anyway.

00:22:49.790 --> 00:22:51.980
And that's what we would
think of as displacement.

00:22:51.980 --> 00:22:54.360
So if you think there's
potential displacement going on,

00:22:54.360 --> 00:22:56.970
and this has actually been well studied

00:22:56.970 --> 00:23:00.767
in the heat and mortality literature,

00:23:00.767 --> 00:23:04.620
you need to also include lags
of your climate variables.

00:23:04.620 --> 00:23:06.850
Again, I didn't wanna go
into too much detail on that,

00:23:06.850 --> 00:23:10.150
but happy to talk about the
statistical details there.

00:23:10.150 --> 00:23:12.800
In any case, we're running
panel fixed regressions,

00:23:12.800 --> 00:23:15.210
we call these distributed lag regressions

00:23:15.210 --> 00:23:17.030
where we're including multiple lags

00:23:17.030 --> 00:23:18.560
of our climate variables,

00:23:18.560 --> 00:23:21.520
and we're adding up
the effects across lags

00:23:21.520 --> 00:23:23.767
to generate the overall effect.

00:23:26.220 --> 00:23:30.620
That was the only statistical
sort of dive I had there,

00:23:30.620 --> 00:23:32.810
again, happy to talk about that more.

00:23:32.810 --> 00:23:34.890
Okay. So quickly why meta-analysis,

00:23:34.890 --> 00:23:36.980
and then I'll show you
some actual results here.

00:23:36.980 --> 00:23:41.130
What can this and what
can't this tell you?

00:23:41.130 --> 00:23:43.240
So it cannot tell you

00:23:43.240 --> 00:23:47.230
anything about specific conflicts.

00:23:47.230 --> 00:23:49.090
So this sort of statistical approach

00:23:49.090 --> 00:23:51.720
and the meta-analysis
we're gonna do on top of it

00:23:51.720 --> 00:23:55.653
can not tell us that climate
caused the Syrian conflict.

00:23:56.730 --> 00:23:57.700
It just can't.

00:23:57.700 --> 00:24:01.940
What it can say instead is, on average,

00:24:01.940 --> 00:24:03.920
do changing climatic conditions

00:24:03.920 --> 00:24:06.430
make conflict more likely.

00:24:06.430 --> 00:24:08.700
And again, typically not
individual conflicts.

00:24:08.700 --> 00:24:11.290
It has little to say often
about individual conflicts

00:24:11.290 --> 00:24:12.930
because the counterfactual,

00:24:12.930 --> 00:24:16.320
the unobserved counterfactual
for that conflict

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:18.590
is, again, unobserved.

00:24:18.590 --> 00:24:21.230
What it can say is, in
years that are hotter,

00:24:21.230 --> 00:24:24.360
drier on average, are you
more likely to see conflicts?

00:24:24.360 --> 00:24:25.810
And does that effect differ

00:24:25.810 --> 00:24:27.910
as a function of other things we observed?

00:24:29.560 --> 00:24:32.440
So that's what we're gonna be able to do.

00:24:32.440 --> 00:24:35.330
Okay. So again, the reason for doing this

00:24:35.330 --> 00:24:37.740
is we think at least threefold,

00:24:37.740 --> 00:24:39.580
this might be able to help us anticipate

00:24:39.580 --> 00:24:41.120
the future impacts of climate change.

00:24:41.120 --> 00:24:43.010
So there's an important
climate change question here,

00:24:43.010 --> 00:24:45.390
and thinking about the overall
cost of climate change,

00:24:45.390 --> 00:24:47.270
or how much we should
mitigate climate change,

00:24:47.270 --> 00:24:51.830
if increased conflict is a
consequence of a warming climate,

00:24:51.830 --> 00:24:53.860
then that's something
we should account for

00:24:53.860 --> 00:24:57.490
when we make policy decisions
about how much to mitigate.

00:24:57.490 --> 00:24:58.550
It might help us understand

00:24:58.550 --> 00:25:01.110
the underlying determinants of conflict.

00:25:01.110 --> 00:25:03.750
If we do see large increases
due to climatic changes,

00:25:03.750 --> 00:25:06.100
that might help us hone our theories

00:25:06.100 --> 00:25:08.980
about why conflicts happen
and what to do about them.

00:25:08.980 --> 00:25:11.470
And again, finally, in
the what to do about them

00:25:11.470 --> 00:25:14.010
since it might help us
identify interventions

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:16.370
that have at least average benefits,

00:25:16.370 --> 00:25:19.680
if not necessarily benefits
for specific conflicts.

00:25:19.680 --> 00:25:22.616
Because again, it's gonna be
hard for us to say something

00:25:22.616 --> 00:25:26.643
statistically responsible
about specific conflicts.

00:25:29.670 --> 00:25:33.510
Okay. So here's our study sample.

