00:28:02.120 --> 00:28:04.570
Okay. So initial questions
we're gonna try to answer,

00:28:04.570 --> 00:28:06.877
what is the average effect of climate?

00:28:06.877 --> 00:28:08.350
And I'm saying that in quotes,

00:28:08.350 --> 00:28:11.110
I think about this is warmer temperatures

00:28:11.110 --> 00:28:12.900
or more extreme rainfall.

00:28:12.900 --> 00:28:16.740
Those are the two main
types of climatic variation

00:28:16.740 --> 00:28:18.300
that we're going to be able to look at.

00:28:18.300 --> 00:28:20.850
So what is the average
effect of a change in climate

00:28:20.850 --> 00:28:23.050
for a given type of conflict?

00:28:23.050 --> 00:28:24.240
And again, we're gonna use

00:28:24.240 --> 00:28:26.230
those three different groups of conflict.

00:28:26.230 --> 00:28:29.370
Number two, do effects
vary across settings?

00:28:29.370 --> 00:28:32.010
So you might see point
estimates in different studies

00:28:32.010 --> 00:28:33.460
that look different.

00:28:33.460 --> 00:28:35.010
Are those truly different,

00:28:35.010 --> 00:28:37.030
or are the differences attributable

00:28:37.030 --> 00:28:40.860
just to sampling variability,
to statistical noise?

00:28:40.860 --> 00:28:43.740
So if we wanna say that
studies are different

00:28:43.740 --> 00:28:44.780
and finding different effects,

00:28:44.780 --> 00:28:47.010
we need to be able to
differentiate those two things.

00:28:47.010 --> 00:28:50.340
True underlying heterogeneity
from sampling variability,

00:28:50.340 --> 00:28:53.420
I'll show you some basic
techniques, we used to do that.

00:28:53.420 --> 00:28:54.920
And finally, can we understand and predict

00:28:54.920 --> 00:28:57.603
why effects vary over settings?

00:28:58.990 --> 00:29:01.460
Okay. So again, three
different types of conflicts.

00:29:01.460 --> 00:29:05.230
I'm gonna show you three
figures that look like this.

00:29:05.230 --> 00:29:08.370
Each line here is a study.

00:29:08.370 --> 00:29:10.010
The print here is very small

00:29:10.010 --> 00:29:12.540
because we have lots of different studies,

00:29:12.540 --> 00:29:14.140
maybe not the best way to show this data.

00:29:14.140 --> 00:29:16.933
So each line here is a study,

00:29:17.860 --> 00:29:21.570
the solid line is the point
estimate of the effect

00:29:21.570 --> 00:29:22.550
from that study,

00:29:22.550 --> 00:29:25.470
the relationship between
climate and conflict.

00:29:25.470 --> 00:29:27.070
You can see the zero line here

00:29:27.070 --> 00:29:30.120
sort of roughly equivalent
to the blue line.

00:29:30.120 --> 00:29:32.590
So anything to the left says

00:29:32.590 --> 00:29:34.310
warming temperatures are more extreme,

00:29:34.310 --> 00:29:36.890
rainfall is reducing the
likelihood of conflict,

00:29:36.890 --> 00:29:39.000
anything to the right of
it is saying the opposite,

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:40.337
warming temperatures increase conflict

00:29:40.337 --> 00:29:44.093
and more extreme rainfall
increases conflict.

00:29:45.420 --> 00:29:47.140
Okay. So this is inter-group conflict.

00:29:47.140 --> 00:29:49.070
And again, we've cast a pretty wide net

00:29:49.070 --> 00:29:51.820
and dumped a lot of things
into this inter-group bucket.

00:29:51.820 --> 00:29:53.560
So we're looking at civil wars,

00:29:53.560 --> 00:29:56.480
we're looking at riots,
we're looking at coups,

00:29:56.480 --> 00:29:58.470
we're looking at terrorist events,

00:29:58.470 --> 00:30:01.603
we're looking at communal,
so non state-based conflict.

00:30:02.750 --> 00:30:06.780
And those were sort of listed
along the outcome column here,

00:30:06.780 --> 00:30:07.950
if you can see that.

00:30:07.950 --> 00:30:09.490
And finally, again,
here are the locations,

00:30:09.490 --> 00:30:12.310
so these vary from pretty
small scale locations,

00:30:12.310 --> 00:30:15.240
so studies just looking at one
corner of Sub-Saharan Africa

00:30:15.240 --> 00:30:17.190
or looking at Syria,

00:30:17.190 --> 00:30:19.560
to global level studies
that use country-level

00:30:19.560 --> 00:30:22.123
or gridded level data
from around the world.

00:30:23.010 --> 00:30:24.430
Okay. So you see the point estimates there

00:30:24.430 --> 00:30:26.110
and underlying the point estimates

00:30:26.110 --> 00:30:27.933
are the confidence interval.

00:30:28.820 --> 00:30:30.870
And so these are bootstrap
confidence intervals

00:30:30.870 --> 00:30:33.540
where each little transparent line

00:30:33.540 --> 00:30:36.313
is a different re-sample
from that same study.

00:30:37.330 --> 00:30:39.770
We are interested in the
statistical uncertainty

00:30:39.770 --> 00:30:40.940
for each of these studies,

00:30:40.940 --> 00:30:43.960
and so we are going to
basically re-sample the data

00:30:43.960 --> 00:30:44.793
from each of these studies

00:30:44.793 --> 00:30:48.740
and re-estimate that
effect many, many times.

00:30:48.740 --> 00:30:53.200
And so studies where the
confidence intervals are narrow

00:30:53.200 --> 00:30:54.170
tend to be ones,

00:30:54.170 --> 00:30:56.270
so here's one, if you can see my cursor,

00:30:56.270 --> 00:31:00.260
where you see a lot of mass
right around the point estimate.

00:31:00.260 --> 00:31:02.380
Many of these other studies,

00:31:02.380 --> 00:31:04.470
especially the ones with
very large effect sizes,

00:31:04.470 --> 00:31:06.060
turn out to be quite uncertain.

00:31:06.060 --> 00:31:07.950
They have large point estimates,

00:31:07.950 --> 00:31:09.870
but the confidence
intervals on these studies

00:31:09.870 --> 00:31:11.113
are quite large.

00:31:12.240 --> 00:31:15.730
And again, I will note that
the estimates shown here

00:31:15.730 --> 00:31:18.100
are often not the ones that you would find

00:31:18.100 --> 00:31:19.540
in these studies themselves.

00:31:19.540 --> 00:31:21.240
We have re-analyzed this data,

00:31:21.240 --> 00:31:23.640
and often we come up with
very different answers

00:31:23.640 --> 00:31:25.540
than what was in the published paper.

00:31:25.540 --> 00:31:28.190
And that's because we are running,

00:31:28.190 --> 00:31:32.520
what we are argue, is the
"correct" statistical analysis

00:31:32.520 --> 00:31:36.330
for studying this relationship.

00:31:36.330 --> 00:31:39.533
So this includes some of
our own earlier studies.

00:31:40.860 --> 00:31:44.280
Here's one of mine, Burke et al, 2009.

00:31:44.280 --> 00:31:46.890
We had a very statistically
significant result

00:31:46.890 --> 00:31:48.230
in that paper that we published,

00:31:48.230 --> 00:31:49.980
we have gone back and re-analyzed it,

00:31:49.980 --> 00:31:52.640
and it is much less
statistically significant

00:31:52.640 --> 00:31:54.340
than what we had published before.

00:31:56.151 --> 00:31:58.530
So we're updating our own work as well.

