maybe they didn't do the best,

00:32:24.550 --> 00:32:28.290
but to what extent are you
kind of like modulating it

00:32:28.290 --> 00:32:29.123
for each study,

00:32:29.123 --> 00:32:31.080
as opposed to just kind
of everyone has to fit

00:32:31.080 --> 00:32:33.970
into your approach.

00:32:33.970 --> 00:32:34.803
- Yes.

00:32:34.803 --> 00:32:35.636
- Just cause that's very different

00:32:35.636 --> 00:32:38.040
from the way you normally
think about a meta-analysis.

00:32:38.040 --> 00:32:39.630
- It is.

00:32:39.630 --> 00:32:41.460
So there are a lot of research

00:32:41.460 --> 00:32:43.570
with degrees of freedom in terms of how

00:32:44.504 --> 00:32:47.140
to code the conflict outcome,

00:32:47.140 --> 00:32:49.570
how to code the climate exposure.

00:32:49.570 --> 00:32:53.150
And so we are letting researchers retain,

00:32:53.150 --> 00:32:56.650
or each paper retain a lot
of those researcher choices

00:32:56.650 --> 00:33:00.910
about how to code conflict,
what conflict to look at,

00:33:00.910 --> 00:33:03.730
what climatic exposure to look at.

00:33:03.730 --> 00:33:05.970
All we're doing is saying,

00:33:05.970 --> 00:33:07.530
you need to have longitudinal data.

00:33:07.530 --> 00:33:10.310
So we've just selected studies
that have longitudinal data

00:33:10.310 --> 00:33:13.593
and you need to plausibly account
for confounding variables.

00:33:14.951 --> 00:33:15.980
And so we're gonna do that

00:33:15.980 --> 00:33:19.130
using some sort of panel
fixed effects analysis.

00:33:19.130 --> 00:33:20.470
Most of these studies

00:33:20.470 --> 00:33:23.390
are doing some version of that already.

00:33:23.390 --> 00:33:26.770
So some of them actually did
not need much alteration.

00:33:26.770 --> 00:33:28.790
Often the only thing we're adding

00:33:28.790 --> 00:33:30.780
is an adding these sort of lag terms,

00:33:30.780 --> 00:33:33.883
that account for displacement
or amplification.

00:33:33.883 --> 00:33:35.470
And in some cases, those
weakened the results,

00:33:35.470 --> 00:33:38.700
in some cases, they actually
strengthened the results.

00:33:38.700 --> 00:33:40.160
So to answer your question, Matt,

00:33:40.160 --> 00:33:41.380
we're trying to strike a balance

00:33:41.380 --> 00:33:44.280
between preserving a lot of the choices,

00:33:44.280 --> 00:33:47.490
the sort of structural
choices the authors made about

00:33:47.490 --> 00:33:50.140
what outcome to look at,
what exposure to look at,

00:33:50.140 --> 00:33:53.970
and we're just trying to
use the same meta-analysis,

00:33:53.970 --> 00:33:57.050
the same statistical sort of framework.

00:33:57.050 --> 00:34:00.930
So yeah, you're right
that this is different

00:34:00.930 --> 00:34:03.520
than some of the
meta-analysis that you see,

00:34:03.520 --> 00:34:07.290
which just take results off the shelf.

00:34:07.290 --> 00:34:10.120
They don't worry about necessarily
the statistical analysis

00:34:10.120 --> 00:34:11.780
that generated the results.

00:34:11.780 --> 00:34:13.860
To us, and especially in this,

00:34:13.860 --> 00:34:17.099
having looked at many,
many, many of these studies,

00:34:17.099 --> 00:34:18.863
that is a mistake,

00:34:19.970 --> 00:34:24.770
because a lot of the variation
for the same dataset,

00:34:24.770 --> 00:34:26.310
you can get very different results

00:34:26.310 --> 00:34:29.490
if given this sort of statistical
choices you make about

00:34:29.490 --> 00:34:30.810
how to run the regression.

00:34:30.810 --> 00:34:33.280
And so to us, a better way of doing this

00:34:33.280 --> 00:34:35.883
is to keep the regression
that we're running fixed,

00:34:37.040 --> 00:34:39.570
and then let authors make choices about

00:34:39.570 --> 00:34:42.170
what outcomes to look at,
what exposures to look at.

