where studies that are more
precise get higher weight.

00:36:48.790 --> 00:36:52.070
This is sort of a standard
approach in meta analysis.

00:36:52.070 --> 00:36:53.610
For precipitation, for rainfall,

00:36:53.610 --> 00:36:56.885
the precision-weighted
mean is dead on zero.

00:36:56.885 --> 00:37:00.610
So it looks like precipitation
sort of on average,

00:37:00.610 --> 00:37:02.350
again, on average has no effect.

00:37:02.350 --> 00:37:03.750
So we get some negative estimates,

00:37:03.750 --> 00:37:05.030
we see some positive estimates,

00:37:05.030 --> 00:37:08.660
the precision weighted mean is zero.

00:37:08.660 --> 00:37:10.030
For temperature it's not zero.

00:37:10.030 --> 00:37:13.670
So we find a precision
weighted effect of about six.

00:37:13.670 --> 00:37:16.040
So sorry, here the units are,

00:37:16.040 --> 00:37:17.510
to make these studies comparable,

00:37:17.510 --> 00:37:18.700
the other thing we have to do

00:37:18.700 --> 00:37:22.260
is convert everything into similar units.

00:37:22.260 --> 00:37:24.910
The units we use are percentage change

00:37:24.910 --> 00:37:26.700
in the conflict outcome

00:37:26.700 --> 00:37:29.530
per standard deviation change
in the climatic exposure.

00:37:29.530 --> 00:37:32.060
So I think that maybe makes sense

00:37:32.060 --> 00:37:33.440
per preset for temperature,

00:37:33.440 --> 00:37:34.880
we don't tend to think of temperature

00:37:34.880 --> 00:37:36.663
in terms of standard deviations.

00:37:38.410 --> 00:37:41.900
So there's that,

00:37:41.900 --> 00:37:44.198
but to make it comparable,
we sort of need to use it.

00:37:44.198 --> 00:37:46.860
So our average temperature effect

00:37:46.860 --> 00:37:49.020
is 6% increase in conflict

00:37:49.020 --> 00:37:51.870
for standard deviation
change in temperature.

00:37:51.870 --> 00:37:55.160
So if you think a really hot year is a two

00:37:55.160 --> 00:37:58.590
or greater standard deviation
increase in temperature,

00:37:58.590 --> 00:38:01.070
that means on average, a
really hot year is associated

00:38:01.070 --> 00:38:06.000
with 10 to 15% increase in conflict.

00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:09.050
So to us that's a meaningful effect.

00:38:09.050 --> 00:38:10.580
That's a pretty large effect.

00:38:10.580 --> 00:38:12.400
Okay. Again, that's the average effect.

00:38:12.400 --> 00:38:13.940
There's a lot of variation.

00:38:13.940 --> 00:38:16.500
Let me come back to that
variation in a moment.

00:38:16.500 --> 00:38:19.660
That's inter-group conflict,
here's interpersonal conflict.

00:38:19.660 --> 00:38:21.930
Here the effects tend to be much smaller,

00:38:21.930 --> 00:38:24.460
but much more precisely estimated.

00:38:24.460 --> 00:38:29.460
A lot of our data here come
from wealthier countries

00:38:30.060 --> 00:38:33.150
where we can put together
pretty detailed data

00:38:33.150 --> 00:38:35.170
on interpersonal conflict.

00:38:35.170 --> 00:38:40.090
So this is often a violent
crime, murder, assault,

00:38:40.090 --> 00:38:43.320
domestic violence, things like that.

00:38:43.320 --> 00:38:44.960
And at least in some settings,

00:38:44.960 --> 00:38:46.170
these are pretty well measured

00:38:46.170 --> 00:38:48.243
in official administrative data.

00:38:49.500 --> 00:38:52.610
So here we have about two dozen studies,

00:38:52.610 --> 00:38:54.390
about 20 studies, I guess, right now,

00:38:54.390 --> 00:38:56.893
25 estimates from almost 20 studies.

00:38:57.930 --> 00:38:59.160
Again, effect sizes are smaller,

00:38:59.160 --> 00:39:01.100
but they're very precisely estimated.

00:39:01.100 --> 00:39:03.420
Most of these appear driven
again by temperature,

00:39:03.420 --> 00:39:04.860
which is the best study relationship.

00:39:04.860 --> 00:39:06.210
So hotter temperatures increase

00:39:06.210 --> 00:39:08.280
the likelihood of interpersonal conflict

00:39:08.280 --> 00:39:10.783
and it's about 2% per standard deviation.

00:39:11.970 --> 00:39:14.720
So again, a very hot
year or very hot week,

00:39:14.720 --> 00:39:17.770
think of it as a two or three
standard deviation event,

00:39:17.770 --> 00:39:19.557
and you get a 6% increase

00:39:19.557 --> 00:39:22.543
in these different types of conflict.

00:39:23.620 --> 00:39:27.240
So again, it's not doubling conflict,

00:39:27.240 --> 00:39:30.190
but it's leading to measurable increases

00:39:30.190 --> 00:39:33.290
in the occurrence of these
different types of conflict.

00:39:33.290 --> 00:39:35.440
So results are actually fairly consistent

00:39:35.440 --> 00:39:37.010
for interpersonal conflict.

00:39:37.010 --> 00:39:38.050
Finally, for self harm,

00:39:38.050 --> 00:39:40.900
and this is the more recent
part of the literature.

00:39:40.900 --> 00:39:45.590
So looking at the relationship
between climatic exposure

00:39:45.590 --> 00:39:47.520
and in the worst case suicide,

00:39:47.520 --> 00:39:49.070
so multiple studies looking at suicide,

00:39:49.070 --> 00:39:50.850
including some work that we've done,

00:39:50.850 --> 00:39:52.253
in the US and Mexico.

00:39:53.947 --> 00:39:56.060
And here again, we see results that look

00:39:56.060 --> 00:39:57.940
more or less like the
interpersonal results

00:39:57.940 --> 00:39:59.120
in terms of their averages.

00:39:59.120 --> 00:40:01.390
So 1% ish increase

00:40:01.390 --> 00:40:04.751
in incidents of various types of self harm

00:40:04.751 --> 00:40:07.860
or negative mental health issues

00:40:08.870 --> 00:40:11.693
per standard deviation
change in temperature.

00:40:13.090 --> 00:40:16.490
Okay. And again, smaller
number of studies here,

00:40:16.490 --> 00:40:19.780
some variation in the point estimates,

00:40:19.780 --> 00:40:22.173
but overall positive relationships.

00:40:23.320 --> 00:40:24.640
Okay.

00:40:24.640 --> 00:40:28.340
So I'm gonna focus mainly on temperature.

00:40:28.340 --> 00:40:31.200
That's where the earlier study,

00:40:31.200 --> 00:40:33.070
our earlier meta-analysis
in our studies here

00:40:33.070 --> 00:40:35.143
showed a more consistent relationship.

00:40:36.400 --> 00:40:39.380
It looks like the mean
effects are positive,

00:40:39.380 --> 00:40:43.320
meaningful in a livelihood
or economic sense,

00:40:43.320 --> 00:40:45.030
but there's a lot of variation.

00:40:45.030 --> 00:40:47.270
And so we first want to understand

00:40:47.270 --> 00:40:48.623
whether this variation

00:40:48.623 --> 00:40:52.760
is representing true
underlying differences

00:40:52.760 --> 00:40:55.140
in the effect sizes across these settings.

00:40:55.140 --> 00:40:58.360
Is it true heterogeneity or
is it just sampling variation?

00:40:58.360 --> 00:41:00.130
Is it just the fact that

00:41:00.130 --> 00:41:01.820
a lot of these estimates are really noisy

00:41:01.820 --> 00:41:03.310
and that we can't really say statistically

00:41:03.310 --> 00:41:06.450
that the effects are
different across settings?

00:41:06.450 --> 00:41:07.800
How do we think about that?

00:41:08.800 --> 00:41:11.630
So again, how do we
separate true heterogeneity

00:41:11.630 --> 00:41:13.550
from sampling variability?

00:41:13.550 --> 00:41:17.130
What we're gonna do here is
estimate hierarchical models.

00:41:17.130 --> 00:41:18.790
If folks are familiar with this,

00:41:18.790 --> 00:41:21.310
economists have just
gotten excited about these

00:41:21.310 --> 00:41:23.030
in the last few years.

00:41:23.030 --> 00:41:25.343
So my bias here is,

00:41:26.197 --> 00:41:27.950
as someone trained as an economist,

00:41:27.950 --> 00:41:30.620
who reads a lot of econ papers,

00:41:30.620 --> 00:41:33.410
a couple of really nice
papers, Rachel Meager,

00:41:33.410 --> 00:41:35.130
Eva Vivalt, in the last couple of years

00:41:35.130 --> 00:41:37.840
have introduced these
techniques to economists,

00:41:37.840 --> 00:41:39.770
but they've really existed for a long time

00:41:39.770 --> 00:41:43.223
in the statistics literature,
Don Rubin, Andrew Gelman,

00:41:44.243 --> 00:41:47.640
and so we're gonna follow their cookbook

00:41:47.640 --> 00:41:49.370
on how to do these things.

00:41:49.370 --> 00:41:51.970
And basically the idea here is,

00:41:51.970 --> 00:41:55.900
and I'm gonna show you a
couple of equations here,

00:41:55.900 --> 00:41:57.760
but it's useful for thinking about,

00:41:57.760 --> 00:41:59.708
and it's really a nice
way to think about effects

00:41:59.708 --> 00:42:04.708
in this or any other meta analysis.

00:42:04.900 --> 00:42:08.750
So imagine we have a
bunch of different studies

00:42:08.750 --> 00:42:11.750
and each has an estimate
of a treatment effect

00:42:11.750 --> 00:42:12.583
you care about.

00:42:12.583 --> 00:42:14.200
So here our treatment effect is

00:42:14.200 --> 00:42:16.720
the impact of a change in
climate on a conflict outcome.

00:42:16.720 --> 00:42:19.860
So each one has an estimate Tk,

00:42:19.860 --> 00:42:22.780
and the hat here means it's an estimate.

00:42:22.780 --> 00:42:26.783
K next to study, so we have
k studies, 68 studies total.

00:42:29.390 --> 00:42:31.630
What we're gonna say in
these hierarchical models

00:42:31.630 --> 00:42:33.370
is the effect that we observed,

00:42:33.370 --> 00:42:35.000
the Tk that we observed

00:42:36.040 --> 00:42:40.090
that is drawn from a distribution

00:42:40.090 --> 00:42:41.530
of some true effects,

00:42:41.530 --> 00:42:43.920
something that we don't observe,

00:42:43.920 --> 00:42:45.550
with some standard error.

00:42:45.550 --> 00:42:49.220
So think about there's
the observed effect,

00:42:49.220 --> 00:42:52.710
and it is an estimate of
what the true effect is

00:42:52.710 --> 00:42:54.500
in that specific setting.

00:42:54.500 --> 00:42:56.700
So I'm studying the impact of climate

00:42:56.700 --> 00:42:58.663
on conflict in Syria.

00:43:00.300 --> 00:43:02.320
I have an estimate from a study

00:43:02.320 --> 00:43:05.040
and that's actually a draw

00:43:05.040 --> 00:43:09.870
from a distribution around a
true unobserved effect, Tk.

00:43:09.870 --> 00:43:11.480
Okay. So that's for each study

00:43:11.480 --> 00:43:13.010
and then each study's effect

00:43:13.010 --> 00:43:16.750
is drawn from abroad or
distribution of true effects.

00:43:16.750 --> 00:43:19.853
And that thing has mean T
and some standard deviation.

00:43:21.870 --> 00:43:24.270
Okay. So we have individual studies

00:43:24.270 --> 00:43:25.407
with true effects that we don't observe,

00:43:25.407 --> 00:43:26.340
and these true effects

00:43:26.340 --> 00:43:28.637
are themselves drawn from
this broader distribution.

00:43:28.637 --> 00:43:31.780
And so the idea here is you
can write these things down

00:43:31.780 --> 00:43:35.080
as normal distributions,

00:43:35.080 --> 00:43:36.680
and you can estimate a model

00:43:36.680 --> 00:43:39.710
that from the data basically learns

00:43:39.710 --> 00:43:43.190
what the true overall average
effect is across the study,

00:43:43.190 --> 00:43:45.860
and whether those effects truly differ,

00:43:45.860 --> 00:43:47.630
so the Sigma square T thing

00:43:47.630 --> 00:43:50.820
is the underlying true variance

00:43:50.820 --> 00:43:52.620
in the effects across the studies.

00:43:52.620 --> 00:43:54.680
And so you can see the hierarchy.

00:43:54.680 --> 00:43:56.640
So this is a two level sort of hierarchy.

00:43:56.640 --> 00:43:59.360
You have the individual level estimates,

00:43:59.360 --> 00:44:03.420
and then you have this
sort of group level means.

00:44:03.420 --> 00:44:04.800
Okay. And basically what
this is gonna tell you,

00:44:04.800 --> 00:44:06.250
when you estimate these things,

00:44:06.250 --> 00:44:08.550
is that if this variance
is greater than zero,

00:44:08.550 --> 00:44:11.968
then there is true
underlying heterogeneity

00:44:11.968 --> 00:44:14.723
in your effects.

00:44:15.600 --> 00:44:18.300
And that's if it's different than zero,

00:44:18.300 --> 00:44:19.620
there's true underlying heterogeneity.

00:44:19.620 --> 00:44:22.620
If it's zero, then that
means all the effects

00:44:22.620 --> 00:44:23.560
have the same mean,

00:44:23.560 --> 00:44:25.020
and that all of the variation

00:44:25.020 --> 00:44:26.710
that you see in the point estimates

00:44:26.710 --> 00:44:28.867
is just driven by sampling noise

00:44:28.867 --> 00:44:31.140
in the sort of first stage,

00:44:31.140 --> 00:44:34.953
in the estimation of individual
level treatment effects.

00:44:36.020 --> 00:44:37.950
This has a nice mapping
to external validity.

00:44:37.950 --> 00:44:40.140
So folks who are used to
thinking about external validity,

00:44:40.140 --> 00:44:43.150
you hear this in applied
micro talks all the time.

00:44:43.150 --> 00:44:45.700
How does your result in setting A,

00:44:45.700 --> 00:44:48.420
what relevance does it
have to new setting B?

00:44:48.420 --> 00:44:50.270
That's an external validity question.

00:44:51.500 --> 00:44:53.930
Again, the variance tells
you something about that.

00:44:53.930 --> 00:44:55.350
If this variance is close to zero,

00:44:55.350 --> 00:44:57.330
then that means the true underlying effect

00:44:57.330 --> 00:44:59.140
does not differ across settings.

00:44:59.140 --> 00:45:01.390
And so you're a study from one setting

00:45:01.390 --> 00:45:03.430
has almost perfect external validity

00:45:03.430 --> 00:45:05.320
to a study from another setting.

00:45:05.320 --> 00:45:06.670
If this thing is large,

00:45:06.670 --> 00:45:08.350
if there's true underlying heterogeneity,

00:45:08.350 --> 00:45:11.000
then you would be worried
about external validity.

00:45:11.000 --> 00:45:13.310
Your study estimate from one setting

00:45:13.310 --> 00:45:15.195
is not gonna be necessarily representative

00:45:15.195 --> 00:45:19.670
of what you might expect
to find in another study.

00:45:19.670 --> 00:45:22.120
This approach gives you a
quantitative way of thinking

00:45:22.120 --> 00:45:23.630
about exactly that question.

00:45:23.630 --> 00:45:24.463
Okay.

00:45:25.440 --> 00:45:27.210
So if the model was right,

00:45:27.210 --> 00:45:28.930
and again, you're writing
these things down as normal,

00:45:28.930 --> 00:45:30.290
is you gotta take some stands on

00:45:30.290 --> 00:45:32.340
sort of what the distributions look like.

00:45:33.600 --> 00:45:36.370
There are some nice things
that come out of it.

00:45:36.370 --> 00:45:38.600
And again, I think of this as a way

00:45:38.600 --> 00:45:42.650
of sort of disciplining our understanding

00:45:42.650 --> 00:45:45.740
of whether these effects
are the same or different.

00:45:45.740 --> 00:45:47.620
So this first thing is it can tell you

00:45:47.620 --> 00:45:49.060
what proportion of the overall

00:45:49.060 --> 00:45:51.200
variation effect sizes across studies

00:45:51.200 --> 00:45:53.650
is just due to sampling noise.

00:45:53.650 --> 00:45:56.040
And that's basically gonna compare

00:45:56.040 --> 00:45:57.770
your estimate of the underlying variance

00:45:57.770 --> 00:46:00.390
to the study specific standard errors.

00:46:00.390 --> 00:46:01.735
So in settings in which

00:46:01.735 --> 00:46:04.330
these standard errors are really large

00:46:04.330 --> 00:46:06.670
and the underlying
variance is really small,

00:46:06.670 --> 00:46:07.790
then you're gonna say

00:46:07.790 --> 00:46:10.610
almost all the underlying variation

00:46:10.610 --> 00:46:13.000
is just coming from sampling noise.

00:46:13.000 --> 00:46:14.550
Versus the setting when
your standard errors

00:46:14.550 --> 00:46:15.650
are really tight,

00:46:15.650 --> 00:46:18.900
your studies are estimated
really precisely,

00:46:18.900 --> 00:46:21.220
but your model says the
underlying variation is large.

00:46:21.220 --> 00:46:22.160
There you're gonna say,

00:46:22.160 --> 00:46:23.550
look, all that variation

00:46:23.550 --> 00:46:26.880
is coming from true
underlying heterogeneity.

00:46:26.880 --> 00:46:29.270
Okay. So the model allows
you to estimate that,

00:46:29.270 --> 00:46:30.990
and also allows you for each study

00:46:30.990 --> 00:46:32.500
to then update your estimate,

00:46:32.500 --> 00:46:33.930
to get a posterior estimate

00:46:33.930 --> 00:46:37.592
of what the actual effect
in that study is gonna be.

00:46:37.592 --> 00:46:39.930
And your estimate,

00:46:39.930 --> 00:46:42.420
your best estimate is gonna
be some weighted average

00:46:42.420 --> 00:46:44.500
of the thing you actually estimated.

00:46:44.500 --> 00:46:48.040
So this is Burke 2009,
the thing we ran before.

00:46:48.040 --> 00:46:49.750
So it's a weighted average of that

00:46:49.750 --> 00:46:52.530
and our estimate of what
the overall average is,

00:46:52.530 --> 00:46:54.670
where the weights are proportional

00:46:54.670 --> 00:46:57.830
to how much sampling noise is in my study.

00:46:57.830 --> 00:46:59.070
So if my study was garbage

00:46:59.070 --> 00:47:00.640
and it was just all sampling noise,

00:47:00.640 --> 00:47:03.370
then we're basically gonna
give my study zero weight,

00:47:03.370 --> 00:47:04.510
and we're gonna say,

00:47:04.510 --> 00:47:06.390
what Marshall should
have found in that paper

00:47:06.390 --> 00:47:09.223
is just the average effect across studies.

00:47:10.380 --> 00:47:12.050
So again, it gives us a way to think about

00:47:12.050 --> 00:47:15.020
not only across the sample,

00:47:15.020 --> 00:47:16.330
where's our variation coming from,

00:47:16.330 --> 00:47:18.000
but for individual studies,

00:47:18.000 --> 00:47:20.690
what was the likely effect
in that individual study,

00:47:20.690 --> 00:47:25.030
given all the other studies
that we observed in the dataset?

00:47:25.030 --> 00:47:27.470
Finally, it gives you a
best guess as to what,

00:47:27.470 --> 00:47:30.305
if you did a new study in a new setting,

00:47:30.305 --> 00:47:34.053
what's your estimate gonna
be in that new setting.

00:47:35.350 --> 00:47:37.390
So it's a really nice, again,

00:47:37.390 --> 00:47:38.750
way to discipline our thinking

00:47:38.750 --> 00:47:42.790
about where is variation in
our effect sizes coming from

00:47:42.790 --> 00:47:45.120
when we look at a lot
of different estimates

00:47:45.120 --> 00:47:46.333
of the same thing.

00:47:47.690 --> 00:47:49.310
I won't talk about estimation here,

00:47:49.310 --> 00:47:51.033
I point you to the Gelman,

00:47:52.760 --> 00:47:54.680
long Gelman chapter

00:47:54.680 --> 00:47:57.827
from which we followed to estimate these,

00:47:57.827 --> 00:48:00.160
and the recent Meager papers,

00:48:00.160 --> 00:48:01.470
if you'd like the econ literature on this,

00:48:01.470 --> 00:48:03.740
which are quite clear on this.

00:48:03.740 --> 00:48:05.040
Okay.

00:48:05.040 --> 00:48:07.440
So we're gonna estimate
these hierarchal models.

00:48:08.349 --> 00:48:12.960
Sorry, I switched the notation here.

00:48:12.960 --> 00:48:15.620
Different papers use different notations.

00:48:15.620 --> 00:48:17.035
So sorry.

00:48:17.035 --> 00:48:20.453
μ is now the mean, I
totally screwed this up.

00:48:20.453 --> 00:48:21.610
μ is now the mean,

00:48:21.610 --> 00:48:23.773
so think of this as the
mean effect across studies,

00:48:23.773 --> 00:48:25.650
T is now the standard deviation.

00:48:25.650 --> 00:48:27.880
So this is the true underlying mean.

00:48:27.880 --> 00:48:30.550
This is the true underlying
standard deviation.

00:48:30.550 --> 00:48:31.680
If T is zero,

00:48:31.680 --> 00:48:34.550
there is no difference in
true effects across studies.

00:48:34.550 --> 00:48:37.143
If T is greater than zero,
there is a difference.

00:48:38.030 --> 00:48:39.710
Okay. So focus again on the inter-group.

00:48:39.710 --> 00:48:41.720
Our mean is close to
what I showed you before,

00:48:41.720 --> 00:48:44.260
so five to six, and again,

00:48:44.260 --> 00:48:47.370
this is in percentage point changes

00:48:47.370 --> 00:48:51.150
per standard deviation
change in climate exposure.

00:48:51.150 --> 00:48:54.290
So a 5% increase in inter-group conflict

00:48:54.290 --> 00:48:58.163
per standard deviation
increase in temperature.

00:48:59.210 --> 00:49:02.070
So that's the mean, what
I showed you before,

00:49:02.070 --> 00:49:03.320
what we would estimate for the model

00:49:03.320 --> 00:49:06.850
is this has substantial true
underlying heterogeneity.

00:49:06.850 --> 00:49:10.450
We can reject that the
standard deviation is zero,

00:49:10.450 --> 00:49:11.343
in fact, it's quite large,

00:49:11.343 --> 00:49:13.220
it's almost as large as the mean.

00:49:13.220 --> 00:49:14.300
So what this would suggest

00:49:14.300 --> 00:49:16.910
is there's a lot of true differences

00:49:16.910 --> 00:49:18.663
across the studies,

00:49:19.770 --> 00:49:23.900
same thing for interpersonal
and same thing for self-harm.

00:49:23.900 --> 00:49:25.790
Although at least for interpersonal,

00:49:25.790 --> 00:49:28.820
the underlying variation
is smaller than the mean.

00:49:28.820 --> 00:49:31.600
So there we saw sort of
tighter standard errors,

00:49:31.600 --> 00:49:34.300
and when you see tighter standard errors

00:49:34.300 --> 00:49:36.650
and the effect sizes are
pretty close together,

00:49:38.660 --> 00:49:41.360
yeah, you can get sort of tighter

00:49:41.360 --> 00:49:43.190
overall distributions here.

00:49:43.190 --> 00:49:45.350
So substantial, but across all of these

00:49:45.350 --> 00:49:48.240
substantial true underlying heterogeneity,

00:49:48.240 --> 00:49:49.920
but at least for inter-group conflict,

00:49:49.920 --> 00:49:52.720
it suggests that at over
half of the overall variation

00:49:52.720 --> 00:49:54.460
and effect sizes that I showed you

00:49:54.460 --> 00:49:55.930
is driven by sampling noise.

00:49:55.930 --> 00:49:57.770
So this pooling estimate suggests

00:49:57.770 --> 00:49:59.930
that 60% of the differences

00:49:59.930 --> 00:50:01.913
are just sampling noise.

00:50:02.820 --> 00:50:04.820
Are just a really wide
confidence intervals

00:50:04.820 --> 00:50:06.950
that we had on each of those studies.

00:50:06.950 --> 00:50:08.120
Less true for interpersonal

00:50:08.120 --> 00:50:09.430
and much less true for self-harm,

00:50:09.430 --> 00:50:10.263
so this suggests that

00:50:10.263 --> 00:50:12.000
actually most of the underlying variation

00:50:12.000 --> 00:50:13.556
is coming from true heterogeneity

00:50:13.556 --> 00:50:15.873
and not just from sampling variable.

00:50:17.380 --> 00:50:19.470
Okay. So what does this tell us?

00:50:19.470 --> 00:50:21.210
This tells us that,

00:50:21.210 --> 00:50:23.890
of those wide variation

00:50:23.890 --> 00:50:26.330
and effect sizes I showed you before,

00:50:26.330 --> 00:50:30.460
they are unlikely, completely
driven by sampling variation.

00:50:30.460 --> 00:50:32.680
There's likely true
underlying heterogeneity,

00:50:32.680 --> 00:50:34.540
and that is particularly true

00:50:34.540 --> 00:50:36.670
for interpersonal and self-harm,

00:50:36.670 --> 00:50:38.540
it's also perhaps somewhat true

00:50:38.540 --> 00:50:39.913
for the inter-group stuff.

00:50:42.350 --> 00:50:43.210
As you can see here,

00:50:43.210 --> 00:50:45.450
maybe 40% of the remaining variation

00:50:45.450 --> 00:50:48.780
is due to true underlying differences.

00:50:48.780 --> 00:50:50.730
Okay. The last thing that
this technology can give you,

00:50:50.730 --> 00:50:53.770
again, is the updated estimates
for individual studies.

00:50:53.770 --> 00:50:56.100
So the black points or
what I showed you before

00:50:56.100 --> 00:50:57.780
for inter-group conflict,

00:50:57.780 --> 00:50:59.320
individual study level estimates,

00:50:59.320 --> 00:51:01.770
the underlying gray line underneath it

00:51:01.770 --> 00:51:03.760
is the standard errors on those estimates,

00:51:03.760 --> 00:51:05.010
the confidence intervals.

00:51:06.110 --> 00:51:08.720
The posterior estimate or
sort of updated estimate

00:51:08.720 --> 00:51:10.520
would be in red.

00:51:10.520 --> 00:51:13.650
So for this Bohlken and
Sergenti, this first study,

00:51:13.650 --> 00:51:17.340
their point estimate was
fairly negative, negative 5%.

00:51:17.340 --> 00:51:19.185
Our BASIE model is gonna tell us

00:51:19.185 --> 00:51:21.920
actually the true estimate in that setting

00:51:21.920 --> 00:51:24.490
is more likely 2%.

00:51:24.490 --> 00:51:26.640
So basically the hierarchical model

00:51:26.640 --> 00:51:30.500
is going to be very skeptical
here of negative estimates,

00:51:30.500 --> 00:51:32.920
negative and particularly
negative noisy estimates.

00:51:32.920 --> 00:51:34.970
It's less skeptical of this one,

00:51:34.970 --> 00:51:36.570
this has a tight standard error.

00:51:37.527 --> 00:51:40.550
But overall it's very
skeptical of noisy estimates,

00:51:40.550 --> 00:51:42.280
and it's going to be
quite skeptical of things

00:51:42.280 --> 00:51:44.890
that are either quite
negative or quite positive.

00:51:44.890 --> 00:51:49.890
And so the term in the literature

00:51:50.360 --> 00:51:52.510
for referring to these
are shrunken estimates,

00:51:52.510 --> 00:51:55.440
often it's pulling these
estimates towards the mean

00:51:55.440 --> 00:51:57.500
and the amount it pulls
them towards the mean

00:51:57.500 --> 00:52:00.920
depends on basically how
uncertain the estimates are.

00:52:00.920 --> 00:52:01.950
So the more uncertain you are,

00:52:01.950 --> 00:52:04.120
the more you get pulled towards mean.

00:52:04.120 --> 00:52:06.830
So if this is right then this says

00:52:06.830 --> 00:52:08.140
it's actually, most of these settings

00:52:08.140 --> 00:52:10.690
were very unlikely to see
the very large estimates

00:52:10.690 --> 00:52:11.760
you get in some of these papers,

00:52:11.760 --> 00:52:14.510
were very unlikely to see,

00:52:14.510 --> 00:52:15.830
unlikely to see large estimates,

00:52:15.830 --> 00:52:19.250
unlikely to see negative estimates at all.

00:52:19.250 --> 00:52:21.870
So only a few of these remain negative

00:52:21.870 --> 00:52:23.963
once we update our priors.

00:52:25.430 --> 00:52:27.233
Okay, so.

00:52:28.930 --> 00:52:32.533
Matt, just making sure we
go for 20 more minutes?

00:52:34.310 --> 00:52:37.893
- I think we go to 3:45.

00:52:37.893 --> 00:52:39.480
- 3:45? Okay.
- Yeah.

00:52:39.480 --> 00:52:41.050
Marshall, just a question about this.

00:52:41.050 --> 00:52:42.620
I mean this sort of shrunken,

00:52:42.620 --> 00:52:44.913
I mean, it's by construction, right?

00:52:46.900 --> 00:52:49.550
I mean, It's always
gonna turn out that way,

00:52:49.550 --> 00:52:50.680
isn't it?

00:52:50.680 --> 00:52:54.880
- So there will always be
a little bit of shrinking

00:52:54.880 --> 00:52:58.083
unless the standard
errors are literally zero.

00:52:59.682 --> 00:53:02.416
So the amount of shrinking

00:53:02.416 --> 00:53:04.820
is going to be a function

00:53:04.820 --> 00:53:07.300
of how uncertain your estimates are.

00:53:07.300 --> 00:53:08.780
So for inter-group conflict,

00:53:08.780 --> 00:53:11.030
we have a lot of really noisy estimates,

00:53:11.030 --> 00:53:13.203
and so you're gonna
get a lot of shrinkage.

00:53:14.640 --> 00:53:17.530
For interpersonal I don't
have that numbers up,

00:53:17.530 --> 00:53:19.670
but there we have a lot of
really tight standard errors,

00:53:19.670 --> 00:53:21.488
and so when you have a
tight standard error,

00:53:21.488 --> 00:53:23.730
the model actually pretty highly weights

00:53:23.730 --> 00:53:25.603
your estimate relative to the mean.

00:53:26.530 --> 00:53:28.170
- Right. But it's like,

00:53:28.170 --> 00:53:31.440
if it's the standard errors
in a study tend to go up

00:53:31.440 --> 00:53:34.090
when your coefficient estimate

00:53:34.090 --> 00:53:35.740
tends to be further from zero,

00:53:35.740 --> 00:53:37.933
it's always gonna shrink it like this.

00:53:39.830 --> 00:53:41.077
That's a question, not a statement.

00:53:41.077 --> 00:53:43.280
- No, no, no, you're you're right.

00:53:43.280 --> 00:53:45.030
But the first part of your question

00:53:46.320 --> 00:53:48.240
doesn't necessarily have to be true.

00:53:48.240 --> 00:53:50.950
It's true under publication bias,

00:53:50.950 --> 00:53:52.460
publication bias story.

00:53:52.460 --> 00:53:54.183
But there shouldn't be a
relationship necessarily

00:53:54.183 --> 00:53:55.580
between the coefficient estimates

00:53:55.580 --> 00:53:57.380
and the size of your standard error.

00:53:59.420 --> 00:54:02.673
There often is because
there is publication bias,

00:54:03.910 --> 00:54:07.933
but there doesn't need to be.

00:54:09.150 --> 00:54:10.500
But you're right, in practice,

00:54:10.500 --> 00:54:12.560
there's basically always shrinking,

00:54:12.560 --> 00:54:14.130
but the shrinking really is a function

00:54:14.130 --> 00:54:16.813
of how noisy each individual estimate is.

00:54:18.060 --> 00:54:22.383
So here we see a lot of
study level shrinkage.

00:54:25.300 --> 00:54:26.850
- Can I jump in with a quick question?

00:54:26.850 --> 00:54:27.813
- Yeah, please.

00:54:28.880 --> 00:54:30.790
- I don't wanna get too deep
in the nitty gritty here,

00:54:30.790 --> 00:54:32.810
but something I didn't understand is,

00:54:32.810 --> 00:54:34.550
are you treating all of these studies

00:54:34.550 --> 00:54:36.380
as if they're drawn sort of IID

00:54:36.380 --> 00:54:38.730
from that normal distribution?

00:54:38.730 --> 00:54:40.330
Are you taking into account

00:54:40.330 --> 00:54:42.020
that there could be some dependence

00:54:42.020 --> 00:54:44.260
between studies in the same regions

00:54:44.260 --> 00:54:49.230
or of the same sub class of violence?

00:54:49.230 --> 00:54:51.650
- Yes. That is a very good question.

00:54:51.650 --> 00:54:53.770
Not witty at all. Yes.

00:54:53.770 --> 00:54:56.040
Right now we are assuming
they are independent,

00:54:56.040 --> 00:54:57.413
which is incorrect.

00:54:59.478 --> 00:55:03.613
So you can build in a covariance between,

00:55:06.320 --> 00:55:08.220
we don't have a good way
of sort of estimating

00:55:08.220 --> 00:55:09.840
how independent they are,

00:55:09.840 --> 00:55:11.010
I think you're absolutely right

00:55:11.010 --> 00:55:13.453
that the assumption of
independence is wrong.

00:55:14.850 --> 00:55:17.100
What I think it will do

00:55:17.100 --> 00:55:18.900
would be to, in this setting,

00:55:18.900 --> 00:55:23.043
lead to further shrinkage, basically.

00:55:25.690 --> 00:55:28.340
So it's a little easier to implement

00:55:28.340 --> 00:55:30.110
in the precision weighted mean setting

00:55:30.110 --> 00:55:32.270
where we can sort of easily assign

00:55:32.270 --> 00:55:33.830
a sort of covariance between studies

00:55:33.830 --> 00:55:36.863
and then calculate the weighted mean.

00:55:39.860 --> 00:55:41.100
Yeah. So you're right.

00:55:41.100 --> 00:55:42.630
Right now we are assuming independence

00:55:42.630 --> 00:55:45.853
and that's likely not correct.

00:55:46.930 --> 00:55:48.920
I think the implication is

00:55:48.920 --> 00:55:50.363
it will shrink things more,

00:55:52.690 --> 00:55:54.730
but yeah, that's a great point.

00:55:54.730 --> 00:55:55.660
- [Student] Thank you.

00:55:55.660 --> 00:55:57.100
- Yeah. No, thank you.

00:55:57.100 --> 00:55:59.180
It's hard to figure out exactly

00:55:59.180 --> 00:56:00.350
how to implement that,

00:56:00.350 --> 00:56:02.310
because you gotta take a stand

00:56:02.310 --> 00:56:03.410
on what that covariance looks like,

00:56:03.410 --> 00:56:05.080
and what, you know,

00:56:05.080 --> 00:56:06.160
do you do it based on

00:56:06.160 --> 00:56:08.510
number of sort of shared observation,

00:56:08.510 --> 00:56:10.603
like how do you do it?

00:56:12.330 --> 00:56:13.163
Yeah.

00:56:13.163 --> 00:56:13.996
So open to ideas,

00:56:13.996 --> 00:56:16.220
if folks have ideas about
the smart way to do that,

00:56:16.220 --> 00:56:19.113
we don't have any.

00:56:21.934 --> 00:56:22.767
Okay.

00:56:24.060 --> 00:56:25.793
So last bit.

00:56:26.680 --> 00:56:29.240
So there is true underlying
heterogeneity, let's say,

00:56:29.240 --> 00:56:30.900
so that just tells us
studies are different.

00:56:30.900 --> 00:56:33.950
It doesn't tell us why
they're different necessarily.

00:56:33.950 --> 00:56:35.240
So can we understand
why they're different?

00:56:35.240 --> 00:56:39.690
So can we explain or
predict this heterogeneity?

00:56:39.690 --> 00:56:41.210
And you could imagine
we could look at this

00:56:41.210 --> 00:56:42.580
in different ways.

00:56:42.580 --> 00:56:45.040
What I showed you before so far

00:56:45.040 --> 00:56:47.260
is the between study differences.

00:56:47.260 --> 00:56:50.180
Do we have these estimates
from different studies?

00:56:50.180 --> 00:56:51.500
We're getting more and more estimates,

00:56:51.500 --> 00:56:53.120
we still don't have that many.

00:56:53.120 --> 00:56:54.540
So again, we have a few dozen

00:56:54.540 --> 00:56:56.840
for these different types of outcomes.

00:56:56.840 --> 00:56:59.760
So you're not super well
powered to understand

00:57:01.730 --> 00:57:04.170
potentially many different
sources of heterogeneity,

00:57:04.170 --> 00:57:05.640
but that's one way of doing it.

00:57:05.640 --> 00:57:06.510
The other way of doing it

00:57:06.510 --> 00:57:08.310
is actually looking within studies.

00:57:08.310 --> 00:57:10.370
So to the extent within different studies,

00:57:10.370 --> 00:57:15.370
we can observe sources of
heterogeneity, moderators.

00:57:17.680 --> 00:57:19.410
Within studies you actually might have

00:57:19.410 --> 00:57:20.830
a lot more data to do that.

00:57:20.830 --> 00:57:24.738
So imagine a lot of
these studies are running

00:57:24.738 --> 00:57:27.480
these longitudinal fixed
effects regressions

00:57:27.480 --> 00:57:28.770
at a grid cell level.

00:57:28.770 --> 00:57:30.790
So you have a grid cell by year,

00:57:30.790 --> 00:57:33.100
and Africa might have tens of thousands

00:57:33.100 --> 00:57:34.620
or hundreds of thousands of grid cells.

00:57:34.620 --> 00:57:36.720
You might have US counties by year

00:57:36.720 --> 00:57:38.490
and have 3,000 counties.

00:57:38.490 --> 00:57:39.960
And so in those settings,

00:57:39.960 --> 00:57:42.740
you have often very rich data

00:57:42.740 --> 00:57:46.700
on moderators of interest.

00:57:46.700 --> 00:57:47.857
And so actually you have a lot more data

00:57:47.857 --> 00:57:49.250
and a lot more statistical power

00:57:49.250 --> 00:57:51.930
to understand heterogeneity.

00:57:51.930 --> 00:57:54.610
So we're actually, so far are gonna focus,

00:57:54.610 --> 00:57:55.777
and what I'll show you today

00:57:55.777 --> 00:57:57.370
is looking within studies,

00:57:57.370 --> 00:57:59.380
these studies where we observe

00:57:59.380 --> 00:58:01.160
some of the moderators
that we're interested in,

00:58:01.160 --> 00:58:03.340
and study whether the effect
of climate on conflict

00:58:03.340 --> 00:58:05.820
differs as a function of those moderators.

00:58:05.820 --> 00:58:07.770
But we have to do both of these things.

00:58:08.610 --> 00:58:10.040
One is just sort of harder

00:58:11.300 --> 00:58:12.402
to figure out how to do it

00:58:12.402 --> 00:58:14.273
in a statistically powered way.

00:58:16.790 --> 00:58:17.623
Okay.

00:58:19.940 --> 00:58:21.700
So far I'm only gonna focus

00:58:21.700 --> 00:58:26.590
on one potential driver of heterogeneity,

00:58:26.590 --> 00:58:28.830
because I'm an economist,

00:58:28.830 --> 00:58:32.120
I'm forced to focus on something
about economic conditions

00:58:32.120 --> 00:58:34.000
as a key moderator.

00:58:34.000 --> 00:58:35.340
So that's what we're gonna do.

00:58:35.340 --> 00:58:36.760
This is, of course,

00:58:36.760 --> 00:58:38.920
an economist favorite
potential moderator here,

00:58:38.920 --> 00:58:41.240
so why might the effect of
climate on conflict differ

00:58:41.240 --> 00:58:42.473
across settings?

00:58:44.470 --> 00:58:46.640
If you believe that economic conditions

00:58:46.640 --> 00:58:49.620
are the thing that links
climate to conflict,

00:58:49.620 --> 00:58:52.583
so you get worsening climatic conditions,

00:58:52.583 --> 00:58:56.430
these change economic
conditions on the ground,

00:58:56.430 --> 00:59:00.420
and these induce individuals
to start or join conflict,

00:59:00.420 --> 00:59:02.100
so that's the typical story

00:59:02.100 --> 00:59:04.740
that you will often see in
the economics literature.

00:59:04.740 --> 00:59:08.570
It's a story about opportunity costs.

00:59:08.570 --> 00:59:13.570
So a worsening climate

00:59:13.870 --> 00:59:16.030
reduces the opportunity cost

00:59:16.030 --> 00:59:17.440
of joining a conflict.

00:59:17.440 --> 00:59:20.190
You need to put food,
put in like normal words,

00:59:20.190 --> 00:59:22.050
you need to put food on the table.

00:59:22.050 --> 00:59:23.330
Your crops have failed.

00:59:23.330 --> 00:59:25.130
This might be the only way to do it.

00:59:26.320 --> 00:59:27.980
These conflicts actually typically start

00:59:27.980 --> 00:59:29.330
with a very small number of people.

00:59:29.330 --> 00:59:30.830
So this doesn't have to turn

00:59:30.830 --> 00:59:32.700
every single farmer into a fighter.

00:59:32.700 --> 00:59:35.570
It just needs to turn a few dozen of them

00:59:35.570 --> 00:59:38.900
into such a thing or have
hundreds join a conflict.

00:59:38.900 --> 00:59:40.690
So just, you need a few at the margin

00:59:40.690 --> 00:59:41.730
to make this decision.

00:59:41.730 --> 00:59:42.733
That's the argument.

00:59:44.450 --> 00:59:46.860
So again, I think there's some
reasonably strong evidence

00:59:46.860 --> 00:59:49.420
of that this is one plausible channel,

00:59:49.420 --> 00:59:51.270
at least in the economics literature.

00:59:52.410 --> 00:59:54.020
You've also seen it in the
individual level conflict.

00:59:54.020 --> 00:59:57.230
So it could be the case that
tough economic conditions

00:59:57.230 --> 00:59:59.150
in a city or a location

00:59:59.150 --> 01:00:01.940
could make individual
level conflict more likely

01:00:02.780 --> 01:00:04.320
when there's, again,

01:00:04.320 --> 01:00:06.380
some sort of downturn or
some sort of triggering event

01:00:06.380 --> 01:00:11.000
brought on by the climate

01:00:11.000 --> 01:00:12.073
or by something else.

01:00:13.610 --> 01:00:15.490
I would say overall that
this sort of hypothesis

01:00:15.490 --> 01:00:17.610
is consistent with the conventional wisdom

01:00:17.610 --> 01:00:19.540
you often see in the
climate impacts literature.

01:00:19.540 --> 01:00:24.010
This is that there's something
insulating about wealth.

01:00:24.010 --> 01:00:27.420
Wealthier populations
are somehow less effected

01:00:27.420 --> 01:00:28.933
by changes in climate.

01:00:30.010 --> 01:00:32.050
I would say this is a
strong conventional wisdom

01:00:32.050 --> 01:00:34.500
that you see all the time.

01:00:34.500 --> 01:00:36.480
I think people, again,
have different stories

01:00:36.480 --> 01:00:37.940
about why this might be the case.

01:00:37.940 --> 01:00:42.160
People can afford to
undertake protective behavior.

01:00:42.160 --> 01:00:44.440
They work in sectors that
are just fundamentally

01:00:44.440 --> 01:00:46.723
less effected by changes
in climate anyway.

01:00:48.260 --> 01:00:50.920
But again, you see various
flavors of this all the time.

01:00:50.920 --> 01:00:53.330
So all of these would predict overall

01:00:53.330 --> 01:00:56.770
that you see a basic heterogeneity

01:00:56.770 --> 01:00:59.710
as a function of economic
conditions on the ground.

01:00:59.710 --> 01:01:01.220
So in wealthier locations,

01:01:01.220 --> 01:01:03.200
you should see less relationship

01:01:03.200 --> 01:01:05.530
between climate and conflict.

01:01:05.530 --> 01:01:06.370
And on some level,

01:01:06.370 --> 01:01:08.140
I think we already know this to be true.

01:01:08.140 --> 01:01:11.060
We have not seen, thank goodness,

01:01:11.060 --> 01:01:12.823
civil conflicts or civil wars

01:01:12.823 --> 01:01:16.540
in most wealthy countries in centuries.

01:01:16.540 --> 01:01:19.200
So if I were to estimate the
relationship between climate

01:01:19.200 --> 01:01:21.360
and civil conflict in
most wealthy countries,

01:01:21.360 --> 01:01:23.060
I would get a zero, right?

01:01:23.060 --> 01:01:25.800
Because we do not observe civil
conflict in those countries.

01:01:25.800 --> 01:01:27.700
So variation in climate,
which we have seen,

01:01:27.700 --> 01:01:30.443
has not induced conflict of that sort.

01:01:31.430 --> 01:01:33.333
So on some level, this has to be true.

01:01:34.830 --> 01:01:36.030
We are going to try to estimate

01:01:36.030 --> 01:01:37.640
on a more local level,

01:01:37.640 --> 01:01:41.030
whether it's true in
settings that we care about.

01:01:41.030 --> 01:01:42.512
Okay.

01:01:42.512 --> 01:01:45.170
- Hey, Marshall, I don't
wanna affect your flow here.

01:01:45.170 --> 01:01:46.050
- No, no, please.

01:01:46.050 --> 01:01:47.410
- You haven't been able to
see in some of the chats,

01:01:47.410 --> 01:01:49.050
but you got a question in here,

01:01:49.050 --> 01:01:51.020
another question, it says,

01:01:51.020 --> 01:01:56.020
this is from Thomas B and he says,

01:01:56.470 --> 01:01:58.800
the linearly vertical
shape of the posterior

01:01:58.800 --> 01:02:00.390
shows both lack of fit

01:02:00.390 --> 01:02:03.640
as well as virtually constant estimates.

01:02:03.640 --> 01:02:06.340
To me, this suggests revisiting
the model assumptions,

01:02:06.340 --> 01:02:09.530
e.g the distribution of normal errors,

01:02:09.530 --> 01:02:11.567
a different distributional
assumption, e.g the YBo

01:02:11.567 --> 01:02:13.833
or the Cauchy might be worth testing.

01:02:15.890 --> 01:02:17.430
- Yeah. I think that's fair.

01:02:17.430 --> 01:02:18.263
Yeah.

01:02:19.650 --> 01:02:20.971
There's some choices you can make

01:02:20.971 --> 01:02:23.573
in the model and Cauchy.

01:02:24.490 --> 01:02:26.813
So the way you, yeah.

01:02:26.813 --> 01:02:29.840
You have to make assumptions or choices

01:02:29.840 --> 01:02:31.050
about what sort of distributions

01:02:31.050 --> 01:02:32.590
you assume about the errors,

01:02:32.590 --> 01:02:34.043
and yeah, I think you're right that,

01:02:34.043 --> 01:02:37.180
that normal might not
be the best choice here.

01:02:37.180 --> 01:02:38.430
Yeah. It's a great point.

01:02:40.580 --> 01:02:43.450
I'm not sure that what I showed

01:02:43.450 --> 01:02:47.133
is necessarily indicative of poor fit.

01:02:47.980 --> 01:02:49.770
I think what it's indicative of,

01:02:49.770 --> 01:02:51.693
is given those assumptions that,

01:02:52.730 --> 01:02:55.560
and given the large statistical noise,

01:02:55.560 --> 01:02:56.630
large confidence intervals

01:02:56.630 --> 01:02:57.610
we see in a lot of these studies,

01:02:57.610 --> 01:02:59.000
we shouldn't be surprised

01:02:59.000 --> 01:03:01.990
that a lot of those get
pulled in towards the mean.

01:03:01.990 --> 01:03:04.320
Just because they're highly uncertain.

01:03:04.320 --> 01:03:07.120
But I think you're right
that we should explore

01:03:07.120 --> 01:03:08.790
whether those results change

01:03:08.790 --> 01:03:10.760
if we change our assumptions

01:03:10.760 --> 01:03:12.350
about the underlying distribution,

01:03:12.350 --> 01:03:14.440
I think that's totally right.

01:03:14.440 --> 01:03:15.273
Thanks.

01:03:17.690 --> 01:03:18.523
Okay.

01:03:19.520 --> 01:03:21.320
So we're gonna do this

01:03:21.320 --> 01:03:22.710
in three different settings right now,

01:03:22.710 --> 01:03:24.080
we're working on expanding this right now.

01:03:24.080 --> 01:03:25.830
This is actually some
primary data analysis.

01:03:25.830 --> 01:03:28.970
So to make sure we can do
this in a consistent way,

01:03:28.970 --> 01:03:30.867
we've gone back and reanalyzed

01:03:32.580 --> 01:03:34.591
gridded data across Sub-Saharan Africa

01:03:34.591 --> 01:03:36.550
over the last three decades.

01:03:36.550 --> 01:03:38.040
This is again, those data from UCDP

01:03:38.040 --> 01:03:39.080
that I showed you before.

01:03:39.080 --> 01:03:41.813
So this is gridded level,
civil conflict data.

01:03:43.160 --> 01:03:47.330
The grid size here is
like a 50 kilometer grid.

01:03:47.330 --> 01:03:51.063
So these are geo referenced
conflict event data.

01:03:52.820 --> 01:03:53.930
So we're gonna look at that

01:03:53.930 --> 01:03:55.380
as our group level conflict outcome,

01:03:55.380 --> 01:03:56.650
and we're gonna look at homicide

01:03:56.650 --> 01:03:58.510
and violent crime in the US

01:03:58.510 --> 01:04:00.850
as our individual level outcomes.

01:04:00.850 --> 01:04:02.280
And again, we're gonna try to estimate

01:04:02.280 --> 01:04:04.790
whether the effect sizes,
the marginal effects,

01:04:04.790 --> 01:04:07.070
so the additional effect
of an increase in climate

01:04:07.070 --> 01:04:08.490
on a conflict outcome,

01:04:08.490 --> 01:04:11.300
either civil conflict or
violent crime in the US,

01:04:11.300 --> 01:04:14.410
whether that differs as a
function of local level income,

01:04:14.410 --> 01:04:15.920
which we observe in the US,

01:04:15.920 --> 01:04:17.710
or local level asset wealth,

01:04:17.710 --> 01:04:22.250
which is the sort of
economic characteristic

01:04:22.250 --> 01:04:24.960
that we observe in Sub-Saharan Africa.

01:04:24.960 --> 01:04:26.393
So the county income level,

01:04:27.510 --> 01:04:31.023
county level income data is
just admin data in the US.

01:04:32.603 --> 01:04:33.960
In a place like Sub-Saharan Africa,

01:04:33.960 --> 01:04:36.530
we often don't have comprehensive
data on these outcomes.

01:04:36.530 --> 01:04:38.260
So the data we are going to use

01:04:38.260 --> 01:04:41.040
comes from a whole different
set of projects that I work on,

01:04:41.040 --> 01:04:42.350
which is a remote sensing,

01:04:42.350 --> 01:04:44.570
machine learning based approach

01:04:44.570 --> 01:04:47.440
to estimating local level livelihoods

01:04:47.440 --> 01:04:48.300
around the world,

01:04:48.300 --> 01:04:50.210
including in Sub-Saharan Africa.

01:04:50.210 --> 01:04:51.290
Happy to talk more about this,

01:04:51.290 --> 01:04:52.860
we have a bunch of papers on this.

01:04:52.860 --> 01:04:56.360
Here's one that we're gonna
use directly from last year

01:04:56.360 --> 01:04:58.033
led by some great students.

01:04:59.978 --> 01:05:02.910
And basically here we're going
to take existing level data

01:05:02.910 --> 01:05:04.790
on economic characteristics on the ground.

01:05:04.790 --> 01:05:07.140
This could be local level asset wealth.

01:05:07.140 --> 01:05:10.450
So think of asset wealth
as a summary index

01:05:10.450 --> 01:05:13.240
of the assets that you own,

01:05:13.240 --> 01:05:16.600
TVs, cars, radio, cell
phones, your roof type,

01:05:16.600 --> 01:05:17.960
what your house is made of,

01:05:17.960 --> 01:05:19.390
whether you have electricity.

01:05:19.390 --> 01:05:21.390
We basically create an index out of that

01:05:22.999 --> 01:05:24.520
and happy to talk more about that,

01:05:24.520 --> 01:05:27.470
that index tends to be pretty robust

01:05:27.470 --> 01:05:28.560
to what we throw in it.

01:05:28.560 --> 01:05:31.063
Ownership of assets tends
to be highly correlated.

01:05:32.170 --> 01:05:34.460
And so we have some surveys
where we observe that,

01:05:34.460 --> 01:05:35.740
and then we're gonna train a model

01:05:35.740 --> 01:05:37.820
to actually predict that
from various sources

01:05:37.820 --> 01:05:39.970
of daytime and nighttime
satellite imagery.

01:05:40.821 --> 01:05:42.630
And what we show in this paper
and another paper is that,

01:05:42.630 --> 01:05:43.640
that does a lot better

01:05:43.640 --> 01:05:45.870
than some of the things you see

01:05:47.650 --> 01:05:49.800
in the econ literature pretty prominently,

01:05:49.800 --> 01:05:51.620
just using nighttime lights imagery.

01:05:51.620 --> 01:05:53.350
So there's a lot of issues
with nighttime lights.

01:05:53.350 --> 01:05:55.770
And it turns out using
that in combination with

01:05:55.770 --> 01:05:57.560
daytime multi-spectral satellite imagery,

01:05:57.560 --> 01:06:00.060
lets you do a very good job

01:06:00.060 --> 01:06:04.210
of predicting variation in asset wealth.

01:06:04.210 --> 01:06:05.980
So here I'm showing you
a figure from this paper.

01:06:05.980 --> 01:06:07.470
This is Nigeria.

01:06:07.470 --> 01:06:10.450
We've done this now for
all of Sub-Saharan Africa

01:06:10.450 --> 01:06:11.690
back to about 2003.

01:06:11.690 --> 01:06:13.180
So we have annual estimates

01:06:13.180 --> 01:06:15.160
of very local level asset wealth

01:06:15.160 --> 01:06:17.667
across Sub-Saharan Africa
for the last two decades.

01:06:17.667 --> 01:06:18.650
And so that's gonna be

01:06:18.650 --> 01:06:21.280
our sort of economic measure

01:06:22.360 --> 01:06:25.740
that we're gonna use as a source
of potential heterogeneity

01:06:25.740 --> 01:06:27.190
in Sub-Saharan Africa.

01:06:27.190 --> 01:06:29.830
So again, happy to talk
more about this dataset,

01:06:29.830 --> 01:06:32.270
but that's what we're gonna be using.

01:06:32.270 --> 01:06:35.830
Okay. So here's the three
different heterogeneity analysis.

01:06:35.830 --> 01:06:37.440
I am looking at the time.

01:06:37.440 --> 01:06:39.733
This is last empirical slide.

01:06:41.620 --> 01:06:43.380
So we see strong,

01:06:43.380 --> 01:06:45.340
so this is just run as
a linear interaction

01:06:45.340 --> 01:06:46.500
what I'm showing you here.

01:06:46.500 --> 01:06:48.954
We can do this non-linearly

01:06:48.954 --> 01:06:50.670
and it gets a little bit noisier,

01:06:50.670 --> 01:06:53.330
but the overall sort of slope is the same.

01:06:53.330 --> 01:06:55.841
Basically at low levels of income,

01:06:55.841 --> 01:06:57.870
we can be quite confident

01:06:57.870 --> 01:07:01.360
that the relationship
is large and positive

01:07:01.360 --> 01:07:03.260
for civil conflict in Sub-Saharan Africa.

01:07:03.260 --> 01:07:05.280
So this is low levels of asset wealth.

01:07:05.280 --> 01:07:06.500
For homicide in the US

01:07:06.500 --> 01:07:08.940
and for violent crime in the US.

01:07:08.940 --> 01:07:11.910
So I have bootstrapped this
regression a bunch of times,

01:07:11.910 --> 01:07:13.840
each line here is a different bootstrap.

01:07:13.840 --> 01:07:15.640
So I think I ran it a thousand times

01:07:15.640 --> 01:07:17.250
for each of these.

01:07:17.250 --> 01:07:19.460
So it gives you a sort of a
sense of the visual uncertainty,

01:07:19.460 --> 01:07:20.650
the dark line, if you can see it,

01:07:20.650 --> 01:07:22.930
is the overall sort of median estimate

01:07:22.930 --> 01:07:24.723
across those bootstraps.

01:07:25.610 --> 01:07:28.390
So the estimates at higher wealth index,

01:07:28.390 --> 01:07:29.340
at higher income levels,

01:07:29.340 --> 01:07:31.513
tend to be not statistically significant.

01:07:32.500 --> 01:07:35.090
So again, strong, positive effects

01:07:35.090 --> 01:07:38.173
at low parts of the asset
or income distribution,

01:07:39.040 --> 01:07:41.350
and then effects then decline towards zero

01:07:41.350 --> 01:07:44.230
at higher incomes or higher
levels of asset wealth

01:07:44.230 --> 01:07:46.760
across both individual level conflicts,

01:07:46.760 --> 01:07:49.330
again, homicide, violent crime,

01:07:49.330 --> 01:07:52.010
and civil conflict in Africa.

01:07:52.010 --> 01:07:54.320
So this is not a meta
analysis of existing studies,

01:07:54.320 --> 01:07:56.130
this is now we're using data

01:07:56.130 --> 01:07:57.420
that had been used in those studies

01:07:57.420 --> 01:07:59.603
and we're doing this ourselves.

01:08:00.780 --> 01:08:03.220
This is consistent with some
great country-level work.

01:08:03.220 --> 01:08:04.920
So there's a couple studies out there,

01:08:04.920 --> 01:08:09.280
a very nice study by Teevrat
Garg and colleagues in Mexico,

01:08:09.280 --> 01:08:12.060
by Thiemo Fetzer in India,

01:08:12.060 --> 01:08:15.600
that leverage various policy experiments

01:08:15.600 --> 01:08:20.040
to show that, as in the Indian case,

01:08:20.040 --> 01:08:21.570
workfare that, NREGA program,

01:08:21.570 --> 01:08:22.870
was rolled out across India,

01:08:22.870 --> 01:08:25.300
this broke the link between
climate and conflict.

01:08:25.300 --> 01:08:28.450
So sort of a direct test of
the income hypothesis here.

01:08:28.450 --> 01:08:33.450
Similarly PROGRESA in Mexico
villages or localities

01:08:33.870 --> 01:08:36.660
that got PROGRESA saw less conflict

01:08:36.660 --> 01:08:39.220
as temperatures increased.

01:08:39.220 --> 01:08:40.630
So think of these results

01:08:40.630 --> 01:08:43.300
as consistent with the few other existing,

01:08:43.300 --> 01:08:46.493
very nice quasi-experimental
papers in the literature.

01:08:47.700 --> 01:08:50.970
So it looks like we do see
strong heterogeneity by income.

01:08:50.970 --> 01:08:53.210
Now I think we should be a little cautious

01:08:53.210 --> 01:08:54.060
about these results,

01:08:54.060 --> 01:08:57.550
because income could be
correlated with other things

01:08:57.550 --> 01:08:59.870
that are driving this heterogeneity.

01:08:59.870 --> 01:09:01.940
So we are not going to
have perfect designs

01:09:01.940 --> 01:09:04.210
to be able to isolate the impact of income

01:09:04.210 --> 01:09:06.283
from other things correlated with income.

01:09:07.237 --> 01:09:10.044
Teevrat and Thiemo and colleagues,

01:09:10.044 --> 01:09:12.830
their designs, I think, are
gonna be a little bit nicer

01:09:12.830 --> 01:09:14.610
than these sort of
cross-sectional interactions

01:09:14.610 --> 01:09:15.840
we have here.

01:09:15.840 --> 01:09:17.242
But again, overall, it's consistent

01:09:17.242 --> 01:09:19.923
with what the literature
seems to be saying here.

01:09:21.240 --> 01:09:25.120
Okay. Let me just finish it up quickly.

01:09:25.120 --> 01:09:27.720
So implications for future conflict.

01:09:27.720 --> 01:09:31.160
So hopefully I've convinced
you that looking backwards,

01:09:31.160 --> 01:09:32.250
at least in some settings,

01:09:32.250 --> 01:09:34.220
we see pretty substantial relationships

01:09:34.220 --> 01:09:38.440
between warming in particular
and various types of conflict,

01:09:38.440 --> 01:09:40.000
but that this effect might differ

01:09:40.000 --> 01:09:41.950
as a function of economic conditions,

01:09:41.950 --> 01:09:44.050
average economic conditions on the ground.

01:09:44.050 --> 01:09:48.120
And so effects seem to be
amplified in lower income,

01:09:48.120 --> 01:09:50.240
lower wealth communities,

01:09:50.240 --> 01:09:53.950
either in Sub-Saharan Africa or in the US.

01:09:53.950 --> 01:09:55.840
Okay. So what do we have
coming down the pipe?

01:09:55.840 --> 01:09:58.660
So we have a lot of warming,
as folks might know,

01:09:58.660 --> 01:10:00.680
we remade this sort of standard,

01:10:00.680 --> 01:10:02.810
how much is the world gonna warm up map

01:10:02.810 --> 01:10:04.260
in terms of standard deviations,

01:10:04.260 --> 01:10:06.170
cause that's how we've done the analysis.

01:10:06.170 --> 01:10:07.600
And especially in the tropics,

01:10:07.600 --> 01:10:10.820
so this is the projected
temperature increase by 2050

01:10:10.820 --> 01:10:12.761
in standard deviations.

01:10:12.761 --> 01:10:14.260
This is a plot we made a few years ago.

01:10:14.260 --> 01:10:16.593
It's probably a little
bit higher right now.

01:10:17.590 --> 01:10:19.140
Temperature projections
have come down a little bit,

01:10:19.140 --> 01:10:20.023
thank goodness.

01:10:21.120 --> 01:10:23.918
But basically what you
see in the tropics is,

01:10:23.918 --> 01:10:25.250
been to the tropics or from the tropics,

01:10:25.250 --> 01:10:27.420
you know the temperature
just doesn't vary that much.

01:10:27.420 --> 01:10:29.630
The year to year variation is quite small,

01:10:29.630 --> 01:10:31.850
and so a degree or two
increase in temperature

01:10:31.850 --> 01:10:34.860
is a huge increase in terms
of standard deviations.

01:10:34.860 --> 01:10:37.720
It could be up to a forth

01:10:37.720 --> 01:10:39.940
standard deviation
increase in temperature.

01:10:39.940 --> 01:10:41.290
Given historical variability,

01:10:41.290 --> 01:10:43.570
that's just really tightened the tropics.

01:10:43.570 --> 01:10:45.030
So in terms of standard deviations,

01:10:45.030 --> 01:10:49.577
we expect massive increases
across the tropics,

01:10:49.577 --> 01:10:52.440
and much lower increases in
the much higher latitudes

01:10:52.440 --> 01:10:54.690
where the year to year
variability, historically,

01:10:54.690 --> 01:10:55.740
has been quite large.

01:10:56.575 --> 01:10:58.200
So even though you're
gonna see more warming

01:10:58.200 --> 01:11:00.360
in the higher latitudes in
terms of standard deviations,

01:11:00.360 --> 01:11:01.603
it's actually smaller.

01:11:02.710 --> 01:11:03.543
Okay.

01:11:04.420 --> 01:11:05.930
So if you believe the recent past

01:11:05.930 --> 01:11:07.120
can be a guide for the future,

01:11:07.120 --> 01:11:08.030
this would suggest

01:11:08.030 --> 01:11:10.750
we have a lot of warming
coming down the pipe.

01:11:10.750 --> 01:11:15.700
Warming's rapidly gonna push
societies and the tropics

01:11:15.700 --> 01:11:18.473
beyond what they've
experienced historically.

01:11:19.460 --> 01:11:22.360
And so we might think that
could exacerbate conflict risk,

01:11:22.360 --> 01:11:23.925
but pushing back the other way is,

01:11:23.925 --> 01:11:25.480
perhaps what I just showed you, right?

01:11:25.480 --> 01:11:27.650
If there is income heterogeneity,

01:11:27.650 --> 01:11:30.060
if we think that rising incomes

01:11:30.060 --> 01:11:33.240
could help break the link
between climate and conflict,

01:11:33.240 --> 01:11:34.970
if development continues in the future,

01:11:34.970 --> 01:11:36.290
as it has in the recent past,

01:11:36.290 --> 01:11:38.480
then maybe that's a force
pushing the other way.

01:11:38.480 --> 01:11:40.630
You have warming pushing in one direction

01:11:40.630 --> 01:11:43.230
and you have development
pushing in another direction.

01:11:43.230 --> 01:11:45.320
So here's a map I just made quickly

01:11:45.320 --> 01:11:47.330
of the African asset wealth data we have,

01:11:47.330 --> 01:11:50.110
this is now aggregated to admin two level,

01:11:50.110 --> 01:11:52.650
but this is basically the growth
in asset wealth over time,

01:11:52.650 --> 01:11:54.500
is estimated from satellites.

01:11:54.500 --> 01:11:56.480
A lot of places are growing really fast.

01:11:56.480 --> 01:11:58.370
You have many parts of east Africa,

01:11:58.370 --> 01:11:59.960
parts of west Africa that are growing

01:11:59.960 --> 01:12:03.000
at 0.2 standard deviations per decade,

01:12:03.000 --> 01:12:05.170
which is really impressive growth.

01:12:05.170 --> 01:12:07.360
So these places are developing quickly

01:12:07.360 --> 01:12:10.300
and our estimates would
suggest that that's going to,

01:12:10.300 --> 01:12:12.120
again, reduce the likelihood

01:12:12.120 --> 01:12:15.990
that a negative climate event
triggers a conflict outcome.

01:12:15.990 --> 01:12:16.823
Okay.

01:12:16.823 --> 01:12:19.470
So as these two forces sort
of pushing against each other,

01:12:19.470 --> 01:12:20.370
as we go forward here,

01:12:20.370 --> 01:12:22.390
our goal is to try to say
something quantitative

01:12:22.390 --> 01:12:24.320
about those two forces

01:12:24.320 --> 01:12:27.503
and what the overall effects
might look like in the future.

01:12:28.680 --> 01:12:31.490
That's not ready for public
consumption yet today.

01:12:31.490 --> 01:12:34.310
So I'll just, I'll leave
you with these maps.

01:12:34.310 --> 01:12:35.380
So let me wrap up here.

01:12:35.380 --> 01:12:38.010
Again, I would say fairly
consistent evidence

01:12:38.010 --> 01:12:40.440
of higher temperatures can
contribute, not always,

01:12:40.440 --> 01:12:42.750
but can contribute to conflict risk.

01:12:42.750 --> 01:12:45.230
Again, we see this

01:12:45.230 --> 01:12:47.410
across many different geographic settings,

01:12:47.410 --> 01:12:49.220
many different types of conflicts.

01:12:49.220 --> 01:12:51.610
And I would say the overall
strength of this evidence

01:12:51.610 --> 01:12:54.130
has grown a little bit
since the last study.

01:12:54.130 --> 01:12:56.670
Our average effects are
maybe a bit smaller,

01:12:56.670 --> 01:12:58.640
but we have a lot more evidence

01:12:58.640 --> 01:13:02.280
saying that they're there.

01:13:02.280 --> 01:13:04.160
I think these effect sizes are meaningful.

01:13:04.160 --> 01:13:07.720
So a five to 10% increase or 5% increase

01:13:07.720 --> 01:13:09.610
per standard deviation is large,

01:13:09.610 --> 01:13:12.660
especially if you think
that future climate change

01:13:12.660 --> 01:13:16.030
is going to lead to a mini
standard deviation increase

01:13:16.030 --> 01:13:17.760
in temperature exposure.

01:13:17.760 --> 01:13:20.190
So a naive multiplication
of those two things

01:13:20.190 --> 01:13:22.543
would suggest large increases in conflict,

01:13:23.620 --> 01:13:26.440
but again, pushing the other
way is economic development.

01:13:26.440 --> 01:13:28.940
And so to me, this is at
least somewhat comforting,

01:13:28.940 --> 01:13:31.220
the fact that economic development

01:13:31.220 --> 01:13:33.190
and the various things that that brings

01:13:33.190 --> 01:13:35.240
can help break the link
between climate and conflict.

01:13:35.240 --> 01:13:37.340
We see some evidence that that's the case.

01:13:39.150 --> 01:13:41.720
So really it's gonna be sort
of a horse race, unfortunately,

01:13:41.720 --> 01:13:43.500
between a warming climate,

01:13:43.500 --> 01:13:46.770
and hopefully developing economies

01:13:48.330 --> 01:13:51.869
and with, I would say, a
sort of unknown net impact

01:13:51.869 --> 01:13:54.320
that we're gonna try to estimate.

01:13:54.320 --> 01:13:56.410
So I will stop there. Thanks
a lot for your comments.

01:13:56.410 --> 01:13:57.370
I have a couple more minutes

01:13:57.370 --> 01:13:59.700
and happy to hear more
comments or questions,

01:13:59.700 --> 01:14:02.140
and appreciate the attendance.

01:14:02.140 --> 01:14:02.973
Thanks.

01:14:04.810 --> 01:14:05.643
- Thanks, Marshall.

01:14:05.643 --> 01:14:07.940
Anybody who has any comments or questions,

01:14:07.940 --> 01:14:10.563
feel free to just unmute
yourself and jump in.

01:14:13.223 --> 01:14:15.315
- I have a question,
Marshall, if you don't mind.

01:14:15.315 --> 01:14:16.850
- Yeah, please.

01:14:16.850 --> 01:14:20.010
- I was wondering if you could do

01:14:20.010 --> 01:14:24.270
some of those plots of
the study effect sizes

01:14:24.270 --> 01:14:25.800
as scatterplots,

01:14:25.800 --> 01:14:27.210
with either precision

01:14:27.210 --> 01:14:29.823
or the power of the study on the y-axis.

01:14:30.720 --> 01:14:33.050
It might be interesting to see

01:14:33.050 --> 01:14:36.970
if there's like a discontinuity near zero

01:14:36.970 --> 01:14:41.660
that would suggest that there's
some file drawer problem

01:14:41.660 --> 01:14:44.237
or selection publication bias

01:14:44.237 --> 01:14:47.273
and which studies are
actually being put out there.

01:14:48.870 --> 01:14:50.340
- Yeah. Thanks.

01:14:50.340 --> 01:14:52.470
That's a great idea.

01:14:52.470 --> 01:14:53.420
I think it would be a nice way

01:14:53.420 --> 01:14:55.260
to just look at the effects overall,

01:14:55.260 --> 01:14:59.180
as a sort of one way of
thinking about publication bias.

01:14:59.180 --> 01:15:00.763
I mean, what's funny is,

01:15:03.080 --> 01:15:04.730
I think that's especially a useful way

01:15:04.730 --> 01:15:06.673
of thinking about the existing estimates

01:15:06.673 --> 01:15:08.580
that are in the published studies.

01:15:08.580 --> 01:15:11.810
We think studies might be
selected on those estimates.

01:15:11.810 --> 01:15:13.210
Often our re-analysis

01:15:13.210 --> 01:15:14.043
leads to estimates that
are quite different

01:15:14.043 --> 01:15:16.010
than what's in the published papers.

01:15:16.010 --> 01:15:19.820
So I think there would be less

01:15:21.060 --> 01:15:22.620
chance for publication bias

01:15:22.620 --> 01:15:23.710
in sort of our re-estimates,

01:15:23.710 --> 01:15:25.230
just because of the fact that

01:15:25.230 --> 01:15:27.480
those were not often
the published estimates.

01:15:28.660 --> 01:15:30.460
But I think you're right,

01:15:30.460 --> 01:15:32.480
there are a bunch of approaches

01:15:32.480 --> 01:15:34.740
for thinking about publication bias

01:15:34.740 --> 01:15:36.040
and the importance of publication bias,

01:15:36.040 --> 01:15:37.610
or the existing of publication bias

01:15:37.610 --> 01:15:40.450
in these meta-analysis, and yeah.

01:15:40.450 --> 01:15:42.530
So we need to do more of that.

01:15:42.530 --> 01:15:43.363
Yeah. Thanks.

01:15:44.890 --> 01:15:46.123
- Anybody else?

01:15:53.400 --> 01:15:54.410
Well, thanks, Marshall.

01:15:54.410 --> 01:15:55.473
Very interesting.

01:15:57.550 --> 01:15:59.230
I think the room here,

01:15:59.230 --> 01:16:00.063
the weather here

01:16:00.063 --> 01:16:01.760
has probably scared people
from the room, I see,

01:16:01.760 --> 01:16:05.260
but you got many interested folks online

01:16:05.260 --> 01:16:07.670
and obviously an important
and interesting topic,

01:16:07.670 --> 01:16:10.760
and so I thank you on
behalf of all the others

01:16:10.760 --> 01:16:12.130
for an interesting seminar,

01:16:12.130 --> 01:16:13.560
we look forward to the future results

01:16:13.560 --> 01:16:16.040
that you have coming down the line.

01:16:16.040 --> 01:16:16.920
- All right. Thanks, Matt.

01:16:16.920 --> 01:16:19.600
Thanks for having me.
Thanks everybody for coming.

01:16:19.600 --> 01:16:20.870
- Take care. Bye bye, everybody.

01:16:20.870 --> 01:16:22.270
